const LESSONS = [{"id":"fi-01","topic":"fixed","title":"Fixed-Income Instrument Features","minutes":15,"objective":"Describe the building blocks of a bond (issuer, maturity, par, coupon, currency), the role of covenants, and how call, put, and conversion provisions shift value between issuer and investor.","summary":"Every fixed-income calculation later in the course starts from a bond's terms. If you can read a bond's features quickly, you can predict how it will be priced, who bears which risk, and what the exam is likely to ask.","sections":[{"heading":"The basic terms of a bond","body":["A bond is a contract in which an **issuer** borrows money and promises to repay it with interest. The investor lending the money is the **bondholder** or creditor. The contract is described by a handful of terms, and nearly every question in this reading is built from them.","The **maturity** is the date on which the final payment is made. The time remaining until then is the tenor, or term to maturity. The **par value** (also called principal, face value, or maturity value) is the amount repaid at maturity and the base on which coupons are calculated. The **coupon rate** is the annual interest rate applied to par, and the **coupon frequency** says how often it is paid. A 6% annual-rate bond with par of 1,000 and semiannual frequency pays 30 twice a year.","The **currency** of the bond matters because both coupons and principal are paid in that currency. A dual-currency bond pays coupons in one currency and principal in another, while a currency option bond lets the holder choose. Finally, bonds with no coupons are **zero-coupon bonds** and are issued at a discount to par, with the whole return coming from the price rising toward par."],"bullets":["Coupon payment per period = par value x coupon rate / frequency.","Par, premium, and discount describe the price relative to par: above par is a premium, below par is a discount.","Yield to maturity is the discount rate that equates the bond's price with the present value of its remaining cash flows."]},{"heading":"Legal structure: indenture, covenants, and the issuer","body":["The legal contract is the **bond indenture** (or trust deed). A trustee, usually a financial institution, monitors compliance on behalf of bondholders. The indenture contains the **covenants**, which are promises the issuer makes to protect lenders.","**Affirmative covenants** say what the issuer must do, such as paying interest and principal on time, maintaining insurance on assets, and complying with laws. **Negative covenants** restrict what the issuer may do, such as limits on additional debt, restrictions on dividends or share buybacks, and limits on selling key assets. Negative covenants are the ones that most directly protect creditors because they stop management from shifting value to shareholders.","The type of issuer also matters for credit analysis. Corporate issuers, sovereign governments, agencies, and special purpose entities each have different sources of repayment. A bond may also be a direct obligation of the issuer or may be backed by specific collateral. Those distinctions return in the later lessons on issuers and credit analysis."]},{"heading":"Contingency provisions: call, put, and conversion","body":["A **contingency provision** is a clause that gives either side a right to act only if a specified event occurs or at the holder's or issuer's option. The important rule is that an option held by the issuer lowers the bond's value to the investor, and an option held by the investor raises it.","A **callable bond** gives the issuer the right to redeem the bond before maturity at a set call price, usually when interest rates have fallen so it can refinance more cheaply. That hurts investors, who must reinvest at lower rates, so callable bonds offer a higher yield than otherwise identical non-callable bonds. Call structures include American style (callable any time after the first call date), European style (only on one date), Bermudan style (on specified dates), and a make-whole call, where the issuer pays the present value of the remaining payments at a spread to a benchmark yield, so the investor is largely compensated for the early redemption.","A **putable bond** gives the investor the right to sell the bond back to the issuer at a set price. It protects the holder if rates rise or credit quality deteriorates, so a putable bond trades at a lower yield than a comparable non-putable bond. A **convertible bond** lets the holder exchange the bond for a specified number of the issuer's shares. The holder gets upside from the equity, so convertibles typically carry lower coupons. Other provisions include warrants attached to a bond and contingent convertible bonds (CoCos), which convert to equity or are written down when a trigger such as a capital ratio breach occurs."],"bullets":["Callable: issuer option, so investor demands a higher yield.","Putable: investor option, so investor accepts a lower yield.","Convertible: investor option into shares, so the coupon is lower."]},{"heading":"Legal, regulatory, and tax considerations","body":["Bonds can be issued in a domestic market, in a foreign market under that market's rules, or in the international (Eurobond) market outside any single country's regulation. Regulation determines the disclosure the issuer must provide and who may buy the bond. Tax treatment also matters: in many jurisdictions interest on certain government or municipal bonds is tax exempt, and the tax-exempt yield is lower than an otherwise similar taxable bond. Original issue discount (the gap between issue price and par on a discount bond) may be treated as interest income by tax authorities, even before it is received in cash."]}],"formulas":[],"example":{"title":"Reading a bond's terms","problem":"A company issues a 10-year bond, par 1,000, 5% coupon paid semiannually, callable at 102 after year 5, with a covenant that limits its debt-to-EBITDA ratio to 4.0. Interest rates fall sharply in year 6. Who benefits from the call provision, and what happens to the issuer's decision?","solution":"The coupon is 1,000 x 5% / 2 = 25 per half year. The issuer holds the call option, so it benefits when rates fall: it can redeem at 1,020 and refinance at a lower coupon. The bondholder loses because the bond is taken away just when its price would otherwise have risen, and the money must be reinvested at lower rates. This is why the callable bond had to offer a higher yield at issue than a comparable non-callable bond. The debt-to-EBITDA limit is a negative covenant that protects holders from the issuer borrowing too much."},"mistakes":["Saying a callable bond is worth more than a non-callable one. The call option belongs to the issuer, so the callable bond is worth less to investors and carries a higher yield.","Confusing the coupon rate with the yield. The coupon is fixed by the contract; the yield changes with the bond's market price.","Treating affirmative covenants as the main creditor protection. The restrictive power comes from negative covenants.","Forgetting to divide the annual coupon by the frequency when computing each payment."],"recap":["A bond is defined by issuer, maturity, par value, coupon rate and frequency, and currency.","Covenants in the indenture protect lenders; negative covenants restrict the issuer's actions.","Issuer options (call) lower the bond's value and raise its yield.","Investor options (put, conversion) raise the bond's value and lower its yield or coupon.","Tax and regulatory setting change the effective return and the investor base."],"quiz":[{"q":"A bond has par value of 1,000, a 6% coupon rate, and pays semiannually. Each coupon payment is:","options":["20","30","60","120"],"answer":1,"why":"1,000 x 6% = 60 per year, divided by 2 payments gives 30 per period."},{"q":"Compared with an otherwise identical non-callable bond, a callable bond will most likely offer:","options":["A lower yield","The same yield","A higher yield","A lower coupon and a lower yield"],"answer":2,"why":"The call option favors the issuer, so investors require extra yield as compensation."},{"q":"Which covenant is a negative covenant?","options":["Maintain insurance on pledged assets","Pay interest and principal on schedule","Do not pay dividends above a set limit","Provide audited financial statements annually"],"answer":2,"why":"A negative covenant restricts the issuer; a dividend cap is a restriction. The other three are things the issuer must do, which are affirmative covenants."},{"q":"A make-whole call provision primarily:","options":["Lets the investor sell the bond back at par","Lets the issuer redeem early at a price based on the present value of remaining payments","Converts the bond into shares","Forces the issuer to redeem at a fixed discount"],"answer":1,"why":"The make-whole price is the present value of the remaining cash flows at a benchmark yield plus a spread, so the investor is compensated for the early redemption."}],"order":1},{"id":"fi-02","topic":"fixed","title":"Fixed-Income Cash Flows and Types","minutes":15,"objective":"Compare the main bond payment structures (bullet, fully amortizing, partially amortizing, zero, step-up, floating, inflation-linked) and explain how each changes the timing of principal and interest.","summary":"Two bonds with the same coupon and maturity can behave very differently if their principal is repaid on different schedules. Knowing the cash flow pattern is the first step to pricing and to judging reinvestment and credit exposure.","sections":[{"heading":"Principal repayment structures","body":["The way principal is returned splits bonds into a few families. In a **bullet bond**, interest is paid periodically and the entire principal is repaid at maturity. This is the standard structure for government and most corporate bonds. The issuer must refinance or have the cash on hand on a single date, so bullet bonds concentrate repayment risk at the end.","In a **fully amortizing bond**, each payment includes interest and a portion of principal, so the balance reaches zero by maturity. Payments are often level (like a standard mortgage), with the principal share rising and the interest share falling over time. A **partially amortizing bond** pays down part of the principal through the term and leaves a **balloon payment** at maturity, which must be refinanced or paid in cash. Because principal is returned gradually, amortizing bonds carry less credit exposure toward the end of their life and their average life is shorter than their stated maturity.","A **sinking fund** provision requires the issuer to retire a portion of the bond issue on a schedule, either by buying bonds in the market or by calling them at a set price. It lowers credit risk by spreading repayment over time, but it creates reinvestment risk for holders whose bonds are retired early."],"bullets":["Bullet: all principal at maturity.","Fully amortizing: balance reaches zero at maturity through scheduled payments.","Partially amortizing: scheduled paydown plus a balloon payment.","Sinking fund: scheduled retirement of part of the issue."]},{"heading":"Coupon structures","body":["A **fixed-rate bond** pays the same coupon each period. A **zero-coupon bond** pays no periodic interest and is issued at a discount to par, so the investor's entire return is the price accretion toward par. A **deferred coupon bond** pays no coupons for an initial period and then pays regular coupons. A **step-up coupon bond** has a coupon that increases on a schedule, which gives the issuer a lower initial cost and may encourage the issuer to refinance before the step.","A **floating-rate note (FRN)** pays a coupon equal to a reference rate plus a spread, for example a 3-month benchmark rate plus 0.80%. The coupon resets each period, so the bond's price stays close to par and interest rate risk is low. The spread compensates for the issuer's credit risk. Floors and caps may limit how low or high the coupon can go.","A **payment-in-kind (PIK) bond** lets the issuer pay interest by issuing more bonds rather than cash, which eases the issuer's cash burden but raises the debt balance and the credit risk."]},{"heading":"Inflation-linked bonds and other structures","body":["**Inflation-linked bonds** protect purchasing power by adjusting cash flows for inflation. In an **indexed-annuity** structure the payment itself is adjusted. In an **indexed zero-coupon** structure the single principal payment is adjusted. In the most common design, **capital-indexed bonds**, the principal is adjusted by an inflation index while the coupon rate stays fixed, so the coupon payment rises with the adjusted principal. In some designs principal cannot fall below par when there is deflation. The real yield on an inflation-linked bond is the return before inflation, and the nominal yield on a regular bond is roughly the real yield plus expected inflation.","Other types include **event-linked** instruments such as catastrophe bonds, whose payments depend on a specified event, and **dual-index** or **sustainability-linked** structures where the coupon steps up if the issuer misses a defined target. The key thing to ask for any structure is: what determines each cash flow, and when does it arrive?"]}],"formulas":["Coupon payment = par x coupon rate / payments per year","Floating-rate coupon = reference rate + quoted margin","Capital-indexed principal = original principal x (index at payment date / index at issue)"],"example":{"title":"Capital-indexed bond cash flows","problem":"An inflation-linked bond has par 1,000, a 2% annual coupon paid annually on adjusted principal, and a 5-year term. The price index is 100 at issue and 104 after the first year. What is the year-1 coupon and the adjusted principal after year 1?","solution":"Adjusted principal after year 1 = 1,000 x 104 / 100 = 1,040. The year-1 coupon is 2% x 1,040 = 20.80. If the index keeps rising, both the coupon and the final principal repayment rise, which is how the bond protects purchasing power."},"mistakes":["Assuming every bond is a bullet. Amortizing and sinking-fund bonds return principal earlier, which shortens average life and changes risk.","Thinking a floating-rate note has no risk. It has little interest rate risk but still carries credit risk, which the spread compensates.","Forgetting that a zero-coupon bond still has an annual return even though it pays no coupons. Its return comes from price accretion.","Applying the coupon to the original principal on a capital-indexed bond instead of the inflation-adjusted principal."],"recap":["Principal can be repaid at the end (bullet), gradually (amortizing), or partly gradually with a balloon payment.","A sinking fund retires part of an issue on a schedule, lowering credit risk and adding reinvestment risk.","Floating-rate notes reset to a reference rate plus a margin, so their price stays near par.","Zero-coupon bonds earn their return from the discount accreting to par.","Inflation-linked bonds adjust principal or payments for inflation to protect purchasing power."],"quiz":[{"q":"A bond pays equal installments of interest and principal so that the balance is zero at maturity. It is:","options":["A bullet bond","A fully amortizing bond","A zero-coupon bond","A step-up bond"],"answer":1,"why":"A bond whose scheduled payments include principal and that reaches a zero balance at maturity is fully amortizing."},{"q":"A floating-rate note that resets every quarter to a reference rate plus 1.00% will most likely trade:","options":["Far below par whenever rates rise","At a large premium when rates rise","Close to par at reset dates","At twice its par value"],"answer":2,"why":"The coupon resets with market rates, so the price stays near par except for changes in credit spread."},{"q":"A capital-indexed bond has par 1,000 and a 3% coupon. After the index rises 5% since issue, the next coupon is:","options":["31.50","35.00","50.00","30.00"],"answer":0,"why":"Adjusted principal is 1,000 x 1.05 = 1,050, and 3% x 1,050 = 31.50."},{"q":"Which statement about a sinking fund is correct?","options":["It makes the bond non-callable","It increases credit risk by delaying repayment","It reduces credit risk but exposes holders to reinvestment risk if their bonds are retired early","It converts the bond to equity"],"answer":2,"why":"Scheduled retirement lowers the amount outstanding at maturity, so credit risk falls, but holders whose bonds are retired must reinvest the proceeds."}],"order":2},{"id":"fi-03","topic":"fixed","title":"Fixed-Income Issuance and Trading","minutes":15,"objective":"Explain how bonds are issued in primary markets and traded in secondary markets, and describe repurchase agreements as a short-term funding tool.","summary":"Bond markets work very differently from stock exchanges. Knowing who issues, how bonds are sold, and where they trade explains why liquidity varies so much across bonds.","sections":[{"heading":"Primary and secondary markets","body":["Bonds are first sold to investors in the **primary market**, where the issuer receives the proceeds, and then trade among investors in the **secondary market**, where the issuer receives nothing. The primary market provides capital, and the secondary market provides liquidity, which in turn affects the yield investors demand when they buy new issues.","In a **public offering** the issuer sells bonds to the general public, usually through investment banks acting as underwriters, after registering with the regulator. In an **underwritten offering** (also called a firm commitment) the underwriter buys the whole issue from the issuer at an agreed price and resells it to investors, bearing the risk that it cannot sell all of it. In a **best-efforts offering**, the bank agrees only to sell as much as it can without taking the unsold risk. A **private placement** sells directly to a small group of institutional investors without public registration, with lighter disclosure but less liquidity. A **shelf registration** lets an issuer register a large amount in advance and sell portions over time as market conditions allow. An **auction** is common for government securities, where bids are collected and allocated by price or yield."],"bullets":["Primary market: issuer receives the money.","Secondary market: investors trade with each other.","Underwritten: the bank takes the placement risk.","Best efforts: the bank only tries to sell the issue."]},{"heading":"How bonds trade: dealers and electronic platforms","body":["Most bonds trade **over the counter (OTC)** rather than on an exchange, with dealers quoting a bid price (what they will pay) and an ask price (what they will sell for). Because there are so many different bonds, many of them trade rarely, and the dealer's ability to hold inventory matters. Government bonds from major issuers trade actively and have narrow bid-ask spreads, while a small corporate issue may trade a few times a month with wide spreads.","Electronic trading platforms and all-to-all trading venues have grown, particularly for liquid bonds, and they improve price transparency. Still, liquidity in bonds is conditional: it can disappear in stressed markets. The **bid-ask spread** is a direct measure of the cost of trading, and it widens as liquidity falls. Trade reporting systems in some markets publish prices after the fact, which helps investors judge fair value."]},{"heading":"Settlement and repurchase agreements","body":["Settlement is when the bond and the cash are exchanged. Government bonds often settle the next business day or on the trade date, while corporate bonds typically settle within a day or two. **Clearing** and **custody** systems reduce counterparty risk by guaranteeing delivery against payment.","A **repurchase agreement (repo)** is a short-term collateralized loan. One party sells a security and agrees to buy it back at a higher price on a later date. The seller is borrowing cash, and the buyer is lending cash against collateral. The price difference is the interest, expressed as the **repo rate**. A **reverse repo** is the same trade seen from the lender's side. Repo is a key source of funding for dealers and leveraged investors, and the rate depends on the credit quality and liquidity of the collateral and on the term of the agreement. A **haircut** reduces the amount lent below the collateral's market value to protect the lender from price declines. In an **overnight repo**, the loan lasts one day; a **term repo** has a longer, fixed maturity."],"bullets":["Repo = sell collateral now, repurchase later at a higher price.","Repo rate = annualized interest on the cash borrowed.","Haircut = collateral value minus loan amount, as protection."]}],"formulas":["Repo interest = loan amount x repo rate x (days / 360)","Repurchase price = loan amount + repo interest"],"example":{"title":"Pricing a short repo","problem":"A dealer borrows 10,000,000 for 30 days in a repo at a 4.80% annual repo rate (360-day basis), posting bonds as collateral. What is the repurchase price?","solution":"Interest = 10,000,000 x 0.048 x 30 / 360 = 40,000. The dealer repurchases the bonds at 10,040,000 on the repo date. The dealer effectively borrowed 10,000,000 for 30 days at 4.80%."},"mistakes":["Thinking the issuer receives money when bonds trade in the secondary market. Only primary-market sales raise capital for the issuer.","Assuming bonds trade on exchanges like stocks. Most trade over the counter through dealers.","Mixing up the direction of repo and reverse repo. The cash borrower is doing a repo; the cash lender is doing a reverse repo.","Using 365 days when the problem specifies a 360-day money market basis."],"recap":["Primary markets raise capital; secondary markets provide liquidity.","Underwriters take placement risk in a firm commitment but not in best-efforts offerings.","Most bonds trade OTC through dealers, and the bid-ask spread measures trading cost.","A repo is a collateralized short-term loan, with interest implied in the repurchase price.","Haircuts protect the lender if the collateral loses value."],"quiz":[{"q":"In an underwritten (firm commitment) offering, the risk of not selling the entire issue is borne by:","options":["The issuer","The investors","The underwriter","The regulator"],"answer":2,"why":"The underwriter buys the issue and resells it, so it bears the unsold-bond risk."},{"q":"A dealer borrows 5,000,000 for 90 days at a repo rate of 3.6% (360-day basis). The repurchase price is:","options":["5,045,000","5,090,000","5,180,000","5,004,500"],"answer":0,"why":"Interest = 5,000,000 x 0.036 x 90 / 360 = 45,000, so the repurchase price is 5,045,000."},{"q":"A lender in a repo agreement lends 95 against collateral worth 100. The difference is called:","options":["A margin call","A haircut","A sinking fund","A make-whole"],"answer":1,"why":"The amount by which the loan is below the collateral value is the haircut, which protects the lender."},{"q":"Which statement about secondary bond markets is correct?","options":["The issuer receives the sale proceeds","Most trading is OTC through dealers","All bonds trade on exchanges","Bid-ask spreads are always the same across bonds"],"answer":1,"why":"Bonds mostly trade over the counter. The issuer gets nothing from secondary trades, and spreads differ with liquidity."}],"order":3},{"id":"fi-04","topic":"fixed","title":"Fixed-Income Markets for Corporate Issuers","minutes":15,"objective":"Describe how corporations raise debt (bank loans, commercial paper, notes and bonds), and rank their debt by security and seniority.","summary":"Companies choose among several debt sources depending on size, credit quality, and how long they need the money. Understanding the menu, and the pecking order of claims in default, is the foundation for corporate credit analysis.","sections":[{"heading":"Short-term funding","body":["Companies need short-term funding to bridge gaps between paying costs and collecting revenue. The common sources are bank credit lines, commercial paper, and other short-term facilities. A **line of credit** is an arrangement with a bank that lets the company borrow up to a set limit. An **uncommitted** line can be withdrawn by the bank, while a **committed** line obliges the bank to lend and the company pays a commitment fee on the unused portion. A **revolving credit facility** is a committed line the company can borrow, repay, and re-borrow over a multi-year term.","**Commercial paper (CP)** is an unsecured short-term promissory note issued by large, creditworthy companies, with maturities usually up to one year. It is sold at a discount to face value, so the investor's return is the difference. Many CP programs are backed by a bank line of credit that acts as a liquidity backstop in case the company cannot roll over its paper. CP is cheaper than a bank loan for strong issuers but disappears for weaker ones when markets are under stress.","A discount-basis instrument is quoted with a discount rate, which is applied to face value, and the investor's actual yield is slightly higher than the quoted discount rate because the discount is earned on a smaller investment."]},{"heading":"Long-term funding: loans and bonds","body":["For long-term needs a company can use a **term loan** from banks, a **syndicated loan** arranged by a group of lenders, or bonds issued to investors. Loans are typically floating-rate, negotiated privately, and may carry tighter covenants. Bonds are tradable securities with fixed or floating rates. **Medium-term notes** are issued continuously in various maturities and sizes under a standing program, so the issuer can tailor a note to a specific investor's request.","Credit quality splits the market in two. **Investment-grade** issuers are rated BBB- or above by S&P and Fitch, or Baa3 or above by Moody's. **High-yield** (speculative-grade or junk) issuers are rated below that. High-yield borrowers tend to pay higher coupons, face tighter covenants, and often rely on bank loans and private markets. Leveraged loans are loans to borrowers that already carry significant debt."],"bullets":["Investment grade: BBB- / Baa3 and above.","High yield: BB+ / Ba1 and below.","Committed lines carry a fee; uncommitted lines can be withdrawn."]},{"heading":"Security and seniority: the ranking of claims","body":["When a company defaults, creditors are repaid according to a **priority of claims**. **Secured debt** is backed by specific collateral, such as property or receivables, so secured lenders can claim that asset first. **Unsecured debt** has no specific collateral and ranks behind secured debt but is still a claim on the company's general assets.","Among unsecured claims there is a ranking by **seniority**: senior unsecured debt is repaid before **subordinated** debt, and all debt is repaid before preferred and common equity. A typical ranking from first to last is first-lien secured debt, second-lien secured debt, senior unsecured debt, subordinated debt, preferred equity, and common equity. In bankruptcy the **absolute priority rule** is meant to follow this order, although in practice negotiated outcomes sometimes deviate.","Because of this ranking, **recovery rates** differ: senior secured creditors typically recover the most and subordinated creditors the least. A bond with lower recovery expectations must offer a higher yield for the same default probability. Structural subordination is another wrinkle: a holding company's creditors are behind the subsidiary's creditors, because the subsidiary's assets must satisfy its own lenders first."],"bullets":["Secured before unsecured; senior before subordinated; debt before equity.","Lower recovery means a higher required yield.","Holding-company debt is structurally subordinated to subsidiary debt."]}],"formulas":["Discount-basis price = face value x (1 - discount rate x days / 360)","Add-on yield = (face value - price) / price x (360 / days)"],"example":{"title":"Commercial paper pricing and yield","problem":"A company issues 90-day commercial paper with face value of 1,000,000 at a quoted discount rate of 4.00% (360-day basis). What is the price, and what is the investor's add-on yield?","solution":"Price = 1,000,000 x (1 - 0.04 x 90 / 360) = 1,000,000 x 0.99 = 990,000. The investor earns 10,000 on 990,000. Add-on yield = 10,000 / 990,000 x 360 / 90 = 4.04%. The add-on yield is higher than the 4.00% discount rate because the return is measured against the smaller price paid."},"mistakes":["Treating the discount rate on commercial paper as the investor's yield. The true yield is slightly higher.","Assuming a secured creditor always recovers fully. Recovery depends on the value of the collateral at the time of default.","Ignoring structural subordination when comparing a parent's bonds with a subsidiary's debt.","Confusing a committed line with an uncommitted one. Only the committed line is a firm obligation of the bank."],"recap":["Short-term funding: credit lines, revolvers, and commercial paper.","Long-term funding: term and syndicated loans, bonds, and medium-term notes.","Investment grade is BBB- / Baa3 or higher; below that is high yield.","Priority of claims: secured, senior unsecured, subordinated, then equity.","Better position in the ranking means higher recovery and a lower required yield."],"quiz":[{"q":"A company issues 180-day commercial paper with face value 1,000,000 at a discount rate of 3.00% (360-day basis). The price is:","options":["970,000","985,000","995,000","1,015,000"],"answer":1,"why":"Price = 1,000,000 x (1 - 0.03 x 180 / 360) = 1,000,000 x 0.985 = 985,000."},{"q":"Which ranking of claims in bankruptcy, from first to last, is most likely correct?","options":["Subordinated debt, senior unsecured, secured, equity","Senior unsecured, secured, subordinated, equity","Secured, senior unsecured, subordinated, equity","Equity, subordinated, senior unsecured, secured"],"answer":2,"why":"Secured claims come first on their collateral, then senior unsecured, then subordinated debt, with equity last."},{"q":"A bond is rated Baa2 by Moody's. It is classified as:","options":["Investment grade","High yield","Defaulted","Unrated"],"answer":0,"why":"Baa3 and above is investment grade, so Baa2 qualifies."},{"q":"Compared with an uncommitted credit line, a committed line:","options":["Can be cancelled by the bank at any time","Obliges the bank to lend and usually charges a commitment fee","Is always unsecured commercial paper","Has no limit on borrowing"],"answer":1,"why":"A committed line is a firm obligation, so the bank charges a fee on the unused portion."}],"order":4},{"id":"fi-05","topic":"fixed","title":"Fixed-Income Markets for Government Issuers","minutes":15,"objective":"Differentiate sovereign, non-sovereign government, quasi-government, supranational, and municipal issuers, and explain how their sources of repayment and credit risk differ.","summary":"Governments and government-related bodies make up a huge share of the bond market, and a sovereign yield is the benchmark everything else is priced against. The exam asks who issues what and what backs the debt.","sections":[{"heading":"Sovereign debt","body":["A **sovereign bond** is issued by a national government to fund its spending and refinance existing debt. Sovereigns issue bills (short maturities, typically a year or less, often zero-coupon), notes, and longer bonds, usually through regular auctions. Because they are large, standardised, and liquid, sovereign yields serve as benchmarks for pricing other bonds, and the spread over the sovereign curve measures credit and liquidity risk.","Credit risk of sovereign debt depends on the government's capacity and willingness to pay. When the government borrows in its own currency, it can in principle create money to pay, so default risk is lower, though that choice can bring inflation and currency depreciation. Governments borrowing in a **foreign currency** have no such option and face higher default risk, since they must earn or acquire that currency. Sovereign repayment is backed by taxing power, the ability to borrow, and, for foreign currency debt, by export earnings and reserves. Analysts also look at the debt-to-GDP ratio, the fiscal deficit, growth prospects, and political stability. Sovereigns can restructure or default, and recoveries are negotiated since there is no standard bankruptcy process.","Sovereign debt is also issued in international markets and in local ones. Many governments issue in a major currency such as the dollar or euro to attract foreign investors, which creates currency exposure in the government's budget."],"bullets":["Bills: short term, usually discount instruments; notes and bonds: pay coupons.","Local-currency debt: the government can print money; foreign-currency debt: it cannot.","Sovereign ratings are typically the ceiling for ratings of domestic issuers, though not always."]},{"heading":"Non-sovereign, quasi-government, and supranational issuers","body":["**Non-sovereign (sub-sovereign) government bonds** are issued by regional or local governments such as states, provinces, and cities. They are usually repaid from the issuer's tax revenue, with the amount of support that the national government gives varying by country. They carry more credit risk than the sovereign.","**Quasi-government entities**, also called agencies, are organisations created or sponsored by a government to carry out a public function, for example agencies that support housing finance or export credit, or government-owned development banks. They may have an explicit guarantee from the national government, or only an implicit one. A bond with an explicit guarantee is similar in credit quality to the sovereign, while one with only implicit support can trade at a wider spread, with the market judging how likely the government is to step in. Some agency debt is issued with no guarantee.","**Supranational agencies** are formed by several countries to promote economic development or cooperation. Examples are the World Bank, the European Investment Bank, and the Asian Development Bank. They fund themselves in capital markets and lend to member countries. Their credit quality is generally very strong, because of high-quality member backing, callable capital commitments from the shareholders, and conservative lending policies, so many are rated AAA and have spreads close to the sovereign curve."]},{"heading":"Municipal bonds","body":["In the United States, **municipal bonds (munis)** are issued by states, cities, counties, and special districts. A feature of the US market is that interest on many municipal bonds is exempt from federal income tax (and sometimes state and local taxes for residents), so munis can offer lower yields than taxable bonds and still appeal to investors in high tax brackets. Compare them using a tax-equivalent yield: taxable-equivalent yield = muni yield / (1 - tax rate).","There are two main types. **General obligation (GO) bonds** are backed by the full faith, credit and taxing power of the issuer and are repaid from general tax revenue. **Revenue bonds** are backed by the revenues of a specific project such as a toll road, airport, or water system. Revenue bonds are riskier, since repayment depends on that project's cash flow, and they often have covenants such as a minimum debt service coverage ratio. Municipal issuers can also default, and these cases include bankruptcy-like proceedings that differ from corporate bankruptcy."],"bullets":["GO bonds: general tax revenue and taxing power.","Revenue bonds: cash flow of a specific project; higher risk than GO bonds of the same issuer.","Tax-equivalent yield = muni yield / (1 - marginal tax rate)."]}],"formulas":["Taxable-equivalent yield = municipal yield / (1 - marginal tax rate)"],"example":{"title":"Municipal versus corporate","problem":"A tax-exempt municipal bond yields 3.2%. An investor's marginal tax rate is 35%. What taxable yield would make a corporate bond equally attractive, and is a corporate bond yielding 4.6% better?","solution":"Taxable-equivalent yield = 3.2% / (1 - 0.35) = 3.2% / 0.65 = 4.92%. The corporate bond at 4.6% pays less than 4.92%, so on an after-tax basis the municipal bond is better, assuming equal credit quality and liquidity."},"mistakes":["Assuming all agency or government-related bonds have an explicit guarantee. Many have only implicit support, which is not the same.","Treating sovereign bonds as risk free. Sovereigns can default, especially on foreign-currency debt.","Forgetting to divide by (1 - tax rate) when finding the taxable-equivalent yield, or multiplying instead.","Treating revenue bonds as backed by the issuer's general taxing power. They depend on the project's revenue."],"recap":["Sovereign yields are benchmarks; credit risk is lower on local-currency debt than on foreign-currency debt.","Sub-sovereign bonds are issued by states, provinces, and cities, and are repaid from taxes or project revenue.","Quasi-government (agency) debt may have explicit or implicit government support.","Supranationals are multi-country institutions with strong credit quality.","Munis: GO bonds rely on taxing power, revenue bonds on a project; tax-equivalent yield = yield / (1 - t)."],"quiz":[{"q":"A 3.2% tax-exempt municipal bond for an investor in a 35% tax bracket has a taxable-equivalent yield of closest to:","options":["2.08%","3.20%","4.92%","5.12%"],"answer":2,"why":"3.2% / (1 - 0.35) = 4.92%. Multiplying 3.2% by (1 - 0.35) gives 2.08%, which reverses the direction."},{"q":"Which issuer is most likely to rely on callable capital from member countries to support its credit quality?","options":["A US city","A supranational agency","A revenue bond issuer","A corporate issuer"],"answer":1,"why":"Supranationals such as the World Bank are owned by member countries, whose commitments to provide capital if needed help support high credit ratings."},{"q":"A government that borrows heavily in a foreign currency, compared with one borrowing in its own currency, most likely has:","options":["Lower default risk","Higher default risk","No exchange rate exposure","The ability to print the needed currency"],"answer":1,"why":"A government cannot print a foreign currency, so repayment depends on earning or acquiring it. That raises default risk relative to local-currency debt."},{"q":"Which municipal bond is repaid from the cash flows of a specific toll road?","options":["General obligation bond","Revenue bond","Treasury bill","Agency bond with explicit guarantee"],"answer":1,"why":"Revenue bonds are backed by the cash flow of the financed project. GO bonds rely on general taxing power."}],"order":5},{"id":"fi-06","topic":"fixed","title":"Fixed-Income Bond Valuation: Prices and Yields","minutes":15,"objective":"Calculate a bond price from its yield, explain why bonds trade at premiums and discounts, handle accrued interest and clean versus dirty prices, and describe matrix pricing.","summary":"Bond valuation is time value of money applied to a known set of cash flows. You will use it constantly in Fixed Income, and the clean-versus-dirty-price distinction is a classic exam trap.","sections":[{"heading":"Price is the present value of the promised cash flows","body":["A bond's value is the sum of the present values of its coupons and its par value, discounted at the market discount rate, which is the yield to maturity (YTM) required by investors for bonds of similar risk. Writing PMT for the coupon per period, FV for par, r for the yield per period, and N for the number of periods: **PV = PMT / (1 + r) + PMT / (1 + r)^2 + ... + (PMT + FV) / (1 + r)^N**. It is an annuity of coupons plus a lump sum.","Keep the period consistent. A bond paying semiannual coupons has 2 periods per year, so a 10-year 5% bond has N = 20, PMT = 2.5 per 100 of par, and r is the annual yield divided by 2. Annual-pay bonds use the annual yield and N equal to the years. On the exam calculator you enter N, I/Y, PMT, and FV and solve for PV, with the sign convention that PV is negative when PMT and FV are positive."],"bullets":["Yields are conventionally quoted as an annual rate with the periodicity of the coupons (a semiannual bond's yield is a 'bond-equivalent' rate).","The inverse relationship: when yield rises, price falls, and vice versa.","The relationship is convex, not linear: a yield decrease raises the price by more than an equal yield increase lowers it."]},{"heading":"Premium, par, and discount","body":["The relationship between the coupon rate and the market yield tells you where the bond trades. If **coupon rate = yield**, the bond prices at **par**. If **coupon rate > yield**, investors receive more than the market requires, so the price is above par: a **premium**. If **coupon rate < yield**, the price is below par: a **discount**. The price is not a statement about quality; it simply reconciles a fixed coupon with a moving market yield.","As time passes with yield unchanged, a premium or discount bond's price is **pulled to par**: it drifts steadily to 100 at maturity, because the remaining cash flows shrink toward par. A discount bond's price rises and a premium bond's price falls, and the effect is not an accident but a consequence of discounting fewer periods. A bond held to maturity ends at par regardless of what happens in between, which is why yield changes matter mostly to those who sell earlier. Zero-coupon bonds always trade at a discount to par before maturity when yields are positive."]},{"heading":"Accrued interest, clean price, and dirty price","body":["Coupons are paid on set dates but a bond can be bought at any time. The seller has earned interest since the last coupon date, so the buyer compensates the seller with **accrued interest (AI)**. The price quoted in the market is the **clean price** (flat price), which excludes accrued interest. The price actually paid is the **full price** or **dirty price** = clean price + accrued interest. Quoting clean prices avoids a sawtooth pattern in which the price would jump down every time a coupon was paid.","Accrued interest = coupon per period x (days since last coupon / days in the coupon period). The day count depends on the market: **30/360** treats each month as 30 days and each year as 360, commonly used for corporate bonds; **actual/actual** uses true days, commonly used for government bonds. Pricing between coupon dates: first discount the cash flows as if settlement were on a coupon date, then multiply by (1 + r) raised to the fraction of the period elapsed. Equivalently, discount each cash flow from its payment date and note that the first coupon is a fraction w = (days to next coupon / days in period) of a period away. The result is the full price; subtract AI for the clean price.","Notice that when a coupon is paid, the dirty price drops by the coupon amount but the clean price barely moves, if yields are constant. It is the clean price that is used to compare bonds and the clean price that rises or falls toward par."]},{"heading":"Matrix pricing","body":["Many bonds, particularly small corporate issues, rarely trade, so there is no recent market yield to use. In **matrix pricing**, the analyst estimates a yield for the illiquid bond from the yields of comparable, actively traded bonds with similar credit quality, coupon, and maturity, usually by interpolating between them, and then adds an adjustment for any liquidity or other differences. The estimated yield is then used to discount the cash flows in the usual way. Matrix pricing is also used for new issues when setting the initial yield."],"bullets":["Step 1: find comparable bonds (same rating and sector, bracketing the maturity).","Step 2: interpolate the yield for the target maturity.","Step 3: price the bond using that yield."]}],"formulas":["Bond price = sum of PMT / (1 + r)^t for t = 1 to N, plus FV / (1 + r)^N","Coupon per period = annual coupon rate x par / periods per year","Accrued interest = coupon per period x (days since last coupon / days in coupon period)","Full (dirty) price = clean (flat) price + accrued interest","Full price between coupon dates = price at last coupon date x (1 + r)^(days elapsed / days in period)"],"example":{"title":"Pricing at a discount, and clean versus dirty","problem":"(a) A 5-year bond with a 6% annual coupon (par 100) is priced to yield 8%. What is its price? (b) A 6% semiannual-pay bond matures on 1 September 2030 and pays coupons on 1 March and 1 September. It is priced to yield 5% and settles on 15 June 2027 (30/360 day count). Find the accrued interest, full price, and clean price.","solution":"(a) PV = 6 / 1.08 + 6 / 1.08^2 + 6 / 1.08^3 + 6 / 1.08^4 + 106 / 1.08^5 = 92.01. The coupon is below the yield, so the bond is at a discount. (b) Coupon per period = 3. Days from 1 March to 15 June on 30/360 = 3 x 30 + 14 = 104 days out of 180. Accrued interest = 3 x 104 / 180 = 1.7333. At the 1 March coupon date, seven coupons remain with a semiannual yield of 2.5%, giving a value of 103.1747. Full price = 103.1747 x 1.025^(104/180) = 104.6572. Clean price = 104.6572 - 1.7333 = 102.9239."},"mistakes":["Using the annual yield and annual number of years for a semiannual bond. Divide the yield by 2 and double the years.","Treating the quoted (clean) price as the cash the buyer pays. At settlement the buyer pays the clean price plus accrued interest.","Thinking a premium bond will keep trading at a premium until maturity. The price is pulled to par over time.","Believing that the price change for a yield rise and fall is symmetric; the price-yield relationship is convex."],"recap":["Bond price = present value of coupons and par at the yield per period.","Coupon > yield: premium; coupon = yield: par; coupon < yield: discount; all pulled to par at maturity.","Full price = clean price + accrued interest, and accrued interest follows the day-count convention.","Quoted prices are clean, which avoids a sawtooth caused by coupon payments.","Matrix pricing estimates the yield of an illiquid bond from comparable liquid bonds."],"quiz":[{"q":"A 10-year, 5% semiannual-pay bond (par 100) is priced to yield 6% (bond-equivalent). Its price is closest to:","options":["92.56","93.00","100.00","107.44"],"answer":0,"why":"N = 20, PMT = 2.5, r = 3% per period, giving PV = 92.56. Because coupon is below yield, it trades at a discount."},{"q":"A bond has a clean price of 98.50 and a 4% semiannual coupon on par of 100. Settlement is 60 days into a 180-day coupon period. The full price is closest to:","options":["98.50","98.83","99.17","100.50"],"answer":2,"why":"Coupon per period = 2.00; AI = 2.00 x 60 / 180 = 0.667. Full price = 98.50 + 0.667 = 99.17."},{"q":"Holding the yield constant, what happens to the price of a bond trading at a premium as it approaches maturity?","options":["It rises toward par","It falls toward par","It stays constant","It rises above its original premium"],"answer":1,"why":"A premium bond's price is pulled to par as the maturity date approaches, falling steadily to par at maturity."},{"q":"A 4-year 6% annual-pay bond has no recent trades. Comparable 3-year and 5-year bonds have yields of 4.0% and 5.0%. Using matrix pricing with linear interpolation, the 4-year bond's price (par 100) is closest to:","options":["100.00","103.37","105.38","106.00"],"answer":2,"why":"The interpolated yield is 4.5%. Price = 6 / 1.045 + 6 / 1.045^2 + 6 / 1.045^3 + 106 / 1.045^4 = 105.38."}],"order":6},{"id":"fi-07","topic":"fixed","title":"Yield and Yield Spread Measures for Fixed-Rate Bonds","minutes":15,"objective":"Calculate and interpret current yield, yield to maturity, yield to call, and yield to worst, and explain what G-spread, I-spread, Z-spread, and option-adjusted spread measure.","summary":"Price tells you what a bond costs; yield tells you what you earn and lets you compare bonds. Spreads then split that yield into a benchmark part and a credit and liquidity part, a core skill for relative-value work and the exam.","sections":[{"heading":"Current yield and yield to maturity","body":["The simplest measure is the **current yield**: annual coupon divided by the current (clean) price. It captures only the income part of return and ignores the gain or loss as the price moves to par, so it is a rough guide. A 6% bond priced at 95 has a current yield of 6 / 95 = 6.32%, which understates the total return because the investor also earns the move from 95 to 100.","The **yield to maturity (YTM)** is the single discount rate that makes the present value of all promised cash flows equal to the bond's price. It is the bond's internal rate of return if the investor buys now and holds to maturity. For the 6% five-year annual bond at 95, solving 95 = 6 / (1 + y) + ... + 106 / (1 + y)^5 gives y = 7.23%, higher than the current yield because it includes the pull to par. There is no closed-form solution; use the calculator's I/Y function.","YTM is an accurate measure of expected return only under three assumptions: the investor **holds to maturity**, the issuer **makes all payments** on time (no default), and all coupons are **reinvested at the YTM**. If reinvestment rates differ or the bond is sold early, realised return will differ. This reinvestment assumption is a recurring exam point."],"bullets":["Premium bond: coupon rate > current yield > YTM.","Discount bond: coupon rate < current yield < YTM.","Par bond: coupon rate = current yield = YTM."]},{"heading":"Periodicity and callable bonds","body":["For a semiannual bond, solving for the yield gives a **periodic rate** per half-year. The market convention is to double it, producing a **bond-equivalent yield (BEY)**, which is an annual rate with a semiannual compounding. For comparison with annual-pay bonds, convert to an **effective annual yield**: (1 + periodic rate)^2 - 1. A 4% semiannual bond priced at 95 with 10 years remaining has a periodic yield of 2.3152%, a BEY of 4.630%, and an effective annual yield of 4.684%. When comparing bonds with different payment frequencies, put them on the same basis first.","A callable bond can be redeemed before maturity, so the YTM may not be the relevant measure. The **yield to call (YTC)** assumes the bond is called on a given call date at the call price, using the call price as the future value and the years to the call date as N. A bond may have several call dates, so the investor computes yields for each. The **yield to worst (YTW)** is the lowest of the YTM and all yields to call, and it is the conservative measure of the return an investor can expect. Premium callable bonds are the ones most likely to be called when yields fall, so their YTW is typically the YTC; for a discount bond the call is unlikely and the YTW is usually the YTM."]},{"heading":"Yield spreads: G, I, Z, and OAS","body":["A **yield spread** is a bond's yield minus a benchmark yield, and it compensates for credit risk, liquidity risk, and for embedded options. Spreads are quoted in basis points (1 bp = 0.01%). Several benchmarks are used.","The **G-spread** is the bond's yield minus the yield on a government bond of the same maturity, interpolating between government bonds if needed. The **I-spread** (interpolated spread) is the bond's yield minus the swap rate (the fixed rate on an interest rate swap) at the bond's maturity, so it measures the spread over the interbank curve. Both compare one yield to one point on a curve.","The **Z-spread** (zero-volatility spread) is a constant spread added to every point on the benchmark **spot curve** such that the present value of the bond's cash flows equals its price. Since it discounts each cash flow at its own spot rate plus the spread, it is more accurate than a single-yield comparison when the curve is steep. The **option-adjusted spread (OAS)** is the Z-spread minus the value of the embedded option expressed in spread terms: for a callable bond, OAS = Z-spread - option value, so the OAS is smaller than the Z-spread. The OAS lets you compare bonds with and without options on the same credit and liquidity basis, and for an option-free bond, OAS equals the Z-spread."],"bullets":["G-spread and I-spread: one yield minus one benchmark yield.","Z-spread: constant spread over the whole spot curve; used for option-free bonds.","OAS: Z-spread adjusted for the cost of the embedded option; callable bond OAS < Z-spread, putable bond OAS > Z-spread."]}],"formulas":["Current yield = annual coupon / current (clean) price","YTM: the rate y that solves price = sum of CF_t / (1 + y)^t","Bond-equivalent yield = periodic rate x periods per year","Effective annual yield = (1 + periodic rate)^m - 1","YTC: solve for y using the call date as N and the call price as FV","YTW = minimum of YTM and all YTCs","G-spread = bond YTM - government YTM at the same maturity","Callable bond: OAS = Z-spread - option value (in spread terms)"],"example":{"title":"YTM versus YTC","problem":"A 10-year 7% semiannual bond (par 100) is callable in 5 years at 103 and trades at 108. Compute the YTM, the YTC (both as bond-equivalent yields), and the YTW. Also compute the current yield.","solution":"Current yield = 7 / 108 = 6.48%. YTM: N = 20, PMT = 3.5, PV = -108, FV = 100 gives a periodic rate of 2.964%, so YTM = 5.93%. YTC: N = 10, PMT = 3.5, PV = -108, FV = 103 gives a periodic rate of 2.834%, so YTC = 5.67%. YTW = lowest = 5.67%. The call is likely because the bond is at a premium, so 5.67% is the more realistic yield."},"mistakes":["Using the current yield as a full measure of return. It ignores the capital gain or loss to par.","Forgetting that YTM assumes coupons are reinvested at the YTM and the bond is held to maturity.","Using the call price as 100 in a YTC calculation. The future value is the call price.","Mixing up OAS and Z-spread for a callable bond: OAS is lower, because the call option's value is removed from the spread."],"recap":["Current yield = coupon / price; it ignores the gain or loss from price convergence to par.","YTM is the bond's IRR and assumes hold to maturity, no default, and reinvestment at the YTM.","YTC uses the call date and call price; YTW is the lowest of YTM and all YTCs.","G-spread and I-spread compare yields; Z-spread adds a constant to every spot rate.","OAS = Z-spread - option value, so a callable bond's OAS is below its Z-spread."],"quiz":[{"q":"A 5-year, 6% annual-pay bond with par of 100 trades at 95. Its current yield is closest to:","options":["5.70%","6.00%","6.32%","7.23%"],"answer":2,"why":"Current yield = 6 / 95 = 6.32%. The 7.23% figure is the YTM, which also includes the gain from 95 to par."},{"q":"A 4% semiannual bond has a periodic yield of 2.3152%. The bond-equivalent yield and effective annual yield are closest to:","options":["4.63% and 4.68%","4.63% and 4.63%","2.32% and 4.68%","4.68% and 4.63%"],"answer":0,"why":"BEY = 2 x 2.3152% = 4.630%. Effective annual yield = 1.023152^2 - 1 = 4.684%."},{"q":"A callable bond's Z-spread is 120 bp and the option is worth 35 bp in spread terms. Its OAS is closest to:","options":["35 bp","85 bp","120 bp","155 bp"],"answer":1,"why":"For a callable bond, OAS = Z-spread - option value = 120 - 35 = 85 bp. The option benefits the issuer, so removing it lowers the spread."},{"q":"Which assumption is NOT required for YTM to equal the realised annualised return?","options":["The bond is held to maturity","The issuer makes all payments on time","Coupons are reinvested at the YTM","The bond is priced at par at purchase"],"answer":3,"why":"YTM applies to bonds at any price. The three requirements are hold to maturity, no default, and reinvestment of coupons at the YTM."}],"order":7},{"id":"fi-08","topic":"fixed","title":"Yield and Yield Spread Measures for Floating-Rate Instruments","minutes":15,"objective":"Compute a floating-rate note coupon, explain quoted margin and discount margin, and convert between discount-rate and add-on money market yields.","summary":"Floating-rate notes and short-term money market instruments follow different rules from fixed-rate bonds. The exam tests the margin vocabulary and the quoting conventions that make bills and CP look different.","sections":[{"heading":"How a floating-rate note works","body":["A **floating-rate note (FRN)** pays a coupon that resets periodically to a **reference rate** plus a fixed spread. The reference rate is a short-term market rate, such as a risk-free overnight rate compounded over the period, or a term rate of three or six months; older contracts used LIBOR, which has been discontinued and replaced by alternative reference rates (the curriculum uses the generic term **market reference rate, MRR**). The fixed spread is the **quoted margin (QM)**, set at issue to reflect the issuer's credit risk. The coupon for a period is: **(MRR + QM) x par / periods per year**. For example, with a 3-month rate of 2.80%, a quoted margin of 0.60%, and par of 1,000,000, the quarterly coupon is (0.0280 + 0.0060) / 4 x 1,000,000 = 8,500.","The reset is usually set at the start of each period and paid at the end, often called setting in advance and paying in arrears. Some FRNs include a **cap**, which limits the coupon from rising above a maximum rate (good for the issuer), a **floor**, which stops the coupon falling below a minimum (good for the investor), or both together in a **collar**. An **inverse floater** has a coupon that moves in the opposite direction to the reference rate, for example a fixed rate minus the reference rate, so its price is very sensitive to rates."]},{"heading":"Discount margin and FRN pricing","body":["Because the coupon resets with the market, an FRN's price stays near par at reset dates, as long as the credit quality is unchanged. If the issuer's credit risk rises, investors want a bigger spread than the quoted margin, and the price falls below par. The spread the market requires is the **discount margin (DM)**, which is the required spread over the reference rate and is the FRN equivalent of a yield spread. Pricing sets future coupons at the current reference rate plus the quoted margin, and discounts at the current reference rate plus the discount margin: **PV = sum of [(MRR + QM) / m x par] / (1 + (MRR + DM) / m)^t + par / (1 + (MRR + DM) / m)^N**.","This gives clear rules. If **DM = QM**, the FRN is priced at **par**. If **DM > QM** (the market demands more spread than the note pays), the price is **below par**. If **DM < QM**, the price is **above par**. Calculating the DM from a given price is a solve-for-yield problem, and for the exam you are usually asked to price given a DM. Note that using a constant MRR for all future periods is a simplifying assumption, as the real future reference rates are unknown."],"bullets":["Quoted margin: set in the contract and fixed for the life (unless the contract says otherwise).","Required (discount) margin: set by the market and changes with credit and liquidity conditions.","A rise in market rates barely changes an FRN's price at reset dates, unlike a fixed-rate bond."]},{"heading":"Money market yields: discount rate and add-on rate","body":["Money market instruments mature in one year or less and are quoted with unusual conventions. Treasury bills and some commercial paper are quoted on a **bank discount basis**: the **discount rate (DR)** is the discount from face value expressed as a percentage of face value and annualised on a 360-day year: **price = face x (1 - DR x days / 360)**. This understates the true return because it divides by the face value instead of the price paid, and uses 360 days.","Instruments such as certificates of deposit and many interbank deposits are quoted on an **add-on basis**: the rate is applied to the amount invested, and the interest is added at maturity. The **add-on rate (AOR)** is the **holding period yield annualised on a 360-day year** (or 365 in some currencies such as sterling): AOR = (HPY) x (360 / days) where HPY = (face - price) / price. To compare, convert a discount rate into price, then into an add-on rate. A **bond-equivalent yield** uses 365 days instead of 360, which allows comparison with bonds: BEY = HPY x 365 / days.","In practice, money market instruments are compared by converting everything to the same basis. Because the discount rate divides by face and not by price, the add-on rate for the same bill is always higher than the discount rate. Money market yields are periodic rates annualised by simple (non-compounded) scaling, so they are not directly comparable with effective annual yields without compounding."]}],"formulas":["FRN coupon = (MRR + quoted margin) x par / periods per year","FRN price = PV of (MRR + QM) coupons and par, discounted at (MRR + DM) / m per period","Bank discount price = face x (1 - DR x days / 360)","Holding period yield = (face - price) / price","Add-on rate = HPY x (360 / days); bond-equivalent yield = HPY x (365 / days)"],"example":{"title":"A discount-basis bill and an FRN","problem":"(a) A 120-day bill is quoted at a discount rate of 4.00%. Find the price per 100 of face value, the holding period yield, the add-on rate (360-day), and the bond-equivalent yield. (b) A 2-year FRN pays semiannual coupons of MRR + 0.50%. Assume MRR stays at 3.00%. If the required discount margin is 0.80%, what is the price per 100?","solution":"(a) Price = 100 x (1 - 0.04 x 120 / 360) = 98.6667. HPY = (100 - 98.6667) / 98.6667 = 1.3514%. Add-on rate = 1.3514% x 360 / 120 = 4.054%. BEY = 1.3514% x 365 / 120 = 4.110%. (b) Coupon = (3.00% + 0.50%) / 2 x 100 = 1.75 per period. Discount rate per period = (3.00% + 0.80%) / 2 = 1.90%. Price = 1.75 / 1.019 + 1.75 / 1.019^2 + 1.75 / 1.019^3 + 101.75 / 1.019^4 = 99.43. Below par because DM (0.80%) exceeds QM (0.50%)."},"mistakes":["Thinking an FRN always trades at exactly par. It does only when the discount margin equals the quoted margin (and it is at a reset date).","Treating the discount rate on a bill as the investor's return. The add-on rate and bond-equivalent yield are higher.","Mixing day-count bases: 360 for the discount and add-on rates, 365 for the bond-equivalent yield.","Confusing a cap and a floor: a cap benefits the issuer by limiting the coupon, a floor benefits the investor."],"recap":["FRN coupon = (reference rate + quoted margin) x par / periods per year, resetting each period.","Quoted margin is contractual; discount margin is what the market requires.","DM = QM gives par; DM > QM gives a price below par; DM < QM gives a price above par.","Bank discount yield uses face value and 360 days, so it understates the return; the add-on rate is higher.","Bond-equivalent yield = HPY x 365 / days."],"quiz":[{"q":"An FRN pays quarterly at the 3-month rate plus a 0.60% quoted margin. The 3-month rate is 2.80% and par is 1,000,000. The coupon for the quarter is:","options":["7,000","8,500","14,000","34,000"],"answer":1,"why":"(0.0280 + 0.0060) / 4 x 1,000,000 = 8,500. 7,000 omits the margin, and 34,000 forgets to divide by 4."},{"q":"An FRN's required discount margin rises above its quoted margin. At the next reset date its price is most likely:","options":["Above par","At par","Below par","Equal to its coupon"],"answer":2,"why":"If the market demands a larger spread than the note pays (DM > QM), the price falls below par. Typical causes are a deterioration in credit quality."},{"q":"A 90-day bill is quoted at a discount rate of 3.60%. Its add-on rate (360-day basis) is closest to:","options":["3.60%","3.63%","3.65%","3.78%"],"answer":1,"why":"Price = 100 x (1 - 0.036 x 90 / 360) = 99.10. HPY = 0.90 / 99.10 = 0.908%, and x 360 / 90 = 3.633%. The add-on rate exceeds the discount rate because it divides by the price, not face value."},{"q":"An FRN with a cap and a floor on its coupon is said to have:","options":["An inverse floater structure","A collar","A step-up coupon","A make-whole call"],"answer":1,"why":"A cap together with a floor creates a collar, bounding the coupon between a minimum and a maximum rate."}],"order":8},{"id":"fi-09","topic":"fixed","title":"The Term Structure of Interest Rates: Spot, Par, and Forward Curves","minutes":15,"objective":"Distinguish spot, par, and forward rates, bootstrap spot rates from par rates, compute forward rates from spot rates, and describe the shapes of the yield curve and the theories that explain them.","summary":"Interest rates depend on maturity, and a single YTM hides that. The term structure lets you price bonds cash flow by cash flow, infer the market's implied future rates, and understand what an inverted curve is saying.","sections":[{"heading":"Three ways to describe the curve","body":["The **term structure of interest rates** describes how yields vary with maturity for bonds of the same credit quality. It is usually shown as a curve with maturity on the horizontal axis. There are three related versions.","A **spot rate** (zero rate) is the yield on a zero-coupon bond for a given maturity: the rate for a single cash flow received at one date. Spot rates are the cleanest discount rates, because each cash flow is discounted at the rate that matches its own timing. A coupon bond can therefore be priced as a package of zeros: **PV = CF1 / (1 + S1) + CF2 / (1 + S2)^2 + ... + (CFN + par) / (1 + SN)^N**. If a coupon bond's market price differs from this value, an arbitrage opportunity exists, such as stripping and reselling the cash flows.","A **par rate** is the coupon rate at which a bond of a given maturity would price at par. It is the YTM of a par bond. Par rates are what you observe for newly issued government bonds. A **forward rate** is an interest rate agreed today for a loan that starts at a future date. It is written f(j, k) for the rate on a k-year loan starting j years from now. Forward rates are not forecasts, but rates implied by today's spot curve through no-arbitrage reasoning."]},{"heading":"Bootstrapping and forward rates","body":["Spot rates cannot always be observed directly, so analysts **bootstrap** them from par rates. Start with the one-year rate, which is both the par rate and the spot rate. For the two-year maturity, a par bond with coupon c satisfies 1 = c x d1 + (1 + c) x d2, where d is the discount factor 1 / (1 + S)^t. Since d1 is known, solve for d2, then S2 = d2^(-1/2) - 1. Repeat for each longer maturity, using the previously found discount factors. In general, d_N = (1 - c_N x sum of earlier discount factors) / (1 + c_N).","Forward rates come from the equivalence of two investment routes. Investing for j + k years at the spot rate must earn the same as investing for j years at the spot rate and then for k years at the forward rate: **(1 + S_(j+k))^(j+k) = (1 + S_j)^j x (1 + f(j, k))^k**. Solving, f(j, k) = [(1 + S_(j+k))^(j+k) / (1 + S_j)^j]^(1/k) - 1. When the spot curve slopes upward, forward rates are above spot rates; when it is inverted, forward rates are below spot rates."],"bullets":["Spot rate: discounts one cash flow at its own maturity.","Par rate: coupon that makes a bond price at par.","Forward rate: today's rate for a future loan, implied by the spot curve.","A forward price is the price of a bond at a future date, computed using forward rates."]},{"heading":"Yield curve shapes and why they occur","body":["The most common shape is a **normal (upward-sloping) curve**, where longer maturities yield more. A **flat curve** has similar yields at all maturities. An **inverted curve** has short-term yields above long-term yields, and it is often associated with expectations of falling rates and weaker growth, which is why it is watched as a recession signal. A **humped curve** rises and then falls.","Four theories try to explain the shape. The **pure expectations theory** says forward rates are unbiased predictions of future spot rates, so the curve shape reflects only expectations: an upward slope means rates are expected to rise. The **liquidity preference theory** adds a **liquidity premium** that grows with maturity, because investors need extra yield to hold riskier, longer bonds, so forward rates exceed expected spot rates and the curve tends to slope upward even if rates are expected to be flat. The **segmented markets theory** says that investors and borrowers have fixed maturity needs, so each segment sets its own rate by supply and demand, and the curve does not link maturities. The **preferred habitat theory** is a middle ground: investors prefer certain maturities but will move to others if compensated with a premium, so yields reflect both expectations and supply and demand across maturities."]}],"formulas":["Bond price using spot rates: PV = sum of CF_t / (1 + S_t)^t","Discount factor: d_t = 1 / (1 + S_t)^t","Bootstrapping par rates: d_N = (1 - c_N x sum of d_1 ... d_(N-1)) / (1 + c_N)","Forward rate: (1 + S_(j+k))^(j+k) = (1 + S_j)^j x (1 + f(j, k))^k"],"example":{"title":"Forwards from spot rates, and bootstrapping","problem":"(a) The 1-, 2-, and 3-year spot rates are 2%, 3%, and 4%. Find f(1,1), f(2,1), and f(1,2). (b) Par rates for 1-, 2-, and 3-year annual-pay bonds are 2%, 3%, and 4%. Bootstrap the 2- and 3-year spot rates.","solution":"(a) f(1,1) = 1.03^2 / 1.02 - 1 = 4.010%. f(2,1) = 1.04^3 / 1.03^2 - 1 = 6.029%. f(1,2) = [1.04^3 / 1.02]^(1/2) - 1 = 5.015%. (b) d1 = 1 / 1.02 = 0.980392. For 2 years: d2 = (1 - 0.03 x 0.980392) / 1.03 = 0.942319, so S2 = 0.942319^(-1/2) - 1 = 3.015%. For 3 years: d3 = (1 - 0.04 x (0.980392 + 0.942319)) / 1.04 = 0.887588, so S3 = 0.887588^(-1/3) - 1 = 4.055%. Spot rates exceed par rates because the curve slopes upward."},"mistakes":["Treating a forward rate as a prediction. It is the break-even rate implied by no arbitrage, and under liquidity preference it is biased above the expected spot rate.","Using simple subtraction for forward rates (for example 3% - 2% = 1%) instead of the compounding relationship.","Using a coupon bond's YTM to discount every cash flow when asked for an arbitrage-free price. Use spot rates for each cash flow.","Mixing up the theories: segmented markets says maturities are unconnected, preferred habitat allows investors to move for a premium."],"recap":["Spot rates discount single cash flows; par rates are YTMs of bonds priced at par; forward rates are implied future rates.","Bootstrapping solves for spot discount factors one maturity at a time from par rates.","(1 + S_(j+k))^(j+k) = (1 + S_j)^j x (1 + f(j,k))^k links spot and forward rates.","An upward-sloping spot curve means forward rates exceed spot rates; an inverted curve means the opposite.","Pure expectations, liquidity preference, segmented markets, and preferred habitat are the four term structure theories."],"quiz":[{"q":"The 1-year spot rate is 2% and the 2-year spot rate is 3%. The one-year forward rate one year from now, f(1,1), is closest to:","options":["1.00%","4.01%","5.00%","6.03%"],"answer":1,"why":"f(1,1) = 1.03^2 / 1.02 - 1 = 4.01%. A simple difference (1.00%) ignores compounding, and 6.03% is f(2,1) from the 2- and 3-year spots."},{"q":"A 2-year par bond pays a 3% annual coupon and the 1-year spot rate is 2%. The 2-year spot rate is closest to:","options":["2.50%","3.00%","3.02%","3.50%"],"answer":2,"why":"d2 = (1 - 0.03 / 1.02) / 1.03 = 0.942319, so S2 = 0.942319^(-1/2) - 1 = 3.015%, slightly above the 3% par rate because the curve slopes up."},{"q":"Which theory says that investors require extra yield for holding longer-maturity bonds, so forward rates overstate expected future spot rates?","options":["Pure expectations","Liquidity preference","Segmented markets","Preferred habitat only"],"answer":1,"why":"Liquidity preference adds a premium that rises with maturity. Pure expectations has no premium, and segmented markets treats maturities as independent."},{"q":"A 2-year 5% annual-pay bond is priced using spot rates of 2% (1 year) and 3% (2 years). Its value per 100 is closest to:","options":["103.87","104.00","105.00","109.00"],"answer":0,"why":"PV = 5 / 1.02 + 105 / 1.03^2 = 4.902 + 98.973 = 103.87. Discounting each cash flow at its own spot rate is the arbitrage-free approach."}],"order":9},{"id":"fi-10","topic":"fixed","title":"Interest Rate Risk and Return","minutes":15,"objective":"Calculate the horizon return on a bond, explain how price risk and reinvestment risk offset each other, and describe why duration is the key measure of interest rate risk.","summary":"A bond's yield is a promise only if you hold it to maturity and reinvest at the same rate. When rates move, what you actually earn depends on how long you hold. This lesson explains why, and sets up duration.","sections":[{"heading":"Where a bond's return comes from","body":["Over any holding period, a bond investor's return has three sources: the **coupons received**, the **reinvestment income** earned by investing those coupons, and the **price change** (the capital gain or loss) at sale or redemption. The yield to maturity combines all three only under strict assumptions: hold to maturity, no default, and every coupon reinvested at the original YTM. In real life, interest rates change after purchase, so realised return rarely equals the YTM.","The **horizon return** (or realised return) measures what you actually earn over a chosen holding period. Compute the total future value at the horizon: coupons plus interest on reinvested coupons, plus the sale price at the horizon date (found by pricing the remaining cash flows at the yield expected then). Then the horizon yield is **(total future value / purchase price)^(1 / years) - 1**, annualised. Do the compounding using the same period convention as the coupons."]},{"heading":"Price risk versus reinvestment risk","body":["A change in market yields affects the investor in two opposite ways. **Price risk** (market price risk) is the effect on the sale price: when yields rise the price falls, which hurts an investor who sells before maturity. **Reinvestment risk** is the effect on interest earned from reinvesting coupons: when yields rise, coupons are reinvested at higher rates, which helps, and when yields fall, reinvestment income suffers. So a rise in yields hurts via price and helps via reinvestment, and a fall does the reverse.","Which effect wins depends on the horizon. For a short horizon, little coupon income has been reinvested, so **price risk dominates**. For a long horizon, many coupons have been reinvested for a long time, so **reinvestment risk dominates**. In between lies a horizon at which the two effects roughly cancel. That break-even point is the bond's **Macaulay duration**: when the investment horizon equals Macaulay duration, the investor is approximately hedged against a one-time parallel yield shift, which is the basis of immunisation covered in later study.","The numbers below show the 5-year 6% annual bond bought at 5% yield (price 104.33; Macaulay duration about 4.48 years) after an immediate yield change to 3% or 7%. With a 1-year horizon, the return is +12.29% if yields fall to 3% but -1.65% if they rise to 7%, a huge difference caused by price risk. With a 5-year horizon the picture flips: the return is 4.79% at 3% and 5.21% at 7%, because reinvestment dominates and higher yields help. With a 4-year horizon, close to the duration, the returns are 5.25% and 4.77%, both close to the original 5% yield, so the risk largely cancels."],"bullets":["Horizon shorter than Macaulay duration: price risk dominates (rising yields hurt).","Horizon longer than Macaulay duration: reinvestment risk dominates (rising yields help).","Horizon about equal to Macaulay duration: the two effects approximately offset."]},{"heading":"Why duration matters","body":["Because yield changes matter so much, investors need a number that tells them how sensitive a bond's price is. **Duration** is that number. **Macaulay duration** is the weighted-average time until a bond's cash flows are received, with weights equal to the present value of each cash flow as a share of price. **Modified duration** converts it into a price sensitivity: **modified duration = Macaulay duration / (1 + y / m)**, where y is the annual yield and m the periods per year. The approximate percentage price change is **-modified duration x change in yield**. For our bond at a 5% yield, Macaulay duration is 4.478 and modified duration is 4.265, so a 0.5 percentage point rise in yield lowers the price by about 4.265 x 0.5% = 2.13% (the exact change is -2.10%, the difference reflecting convexity).","Several factors drive interest rate risk. **Longer maturity** raises it, because more distant cash flows are discounted over more periods. **Lower coupon** raises it, because more of the value arrives at the end. **Lower yield level** raises it, for a given maturity and coupon. Embedded options change the picture: a callable bond has less price appreciation when yields fall than an otherwise identical straight bond, because call risk caps the price. A zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration equals its maturity, which is the longest for any bond of that maturity."]}],"formulas":["Horizon return (annualised) = (total future value at horizon / purchase price)^(1 / years) - 1","Total future value = coupons + interest on reinvested coupons + sale price at horizon","Modified duration = Macaulay duration / (1 + y / m)","Approximate % price change = -modified duration x change in yield"],"example":{"title":"Horizon return on a 5-year bond","problem":"You buy a 5-year, 6% annual-pay bond (par 100) at a yield of 5%, and hold it for 3 years. Coupons are reinvested at 4%, and at the end of year 3 the bond is sold at a yield of 5.5%. What is the annualised horizon return?","solution":"Purchase price = 6 / 1.05 + 6 / 1.05^2 + 6 / 1.05^3 + 6 / 1.05^4 + 106 / 1.05^5 = 104.3295. Reinvested coupons at year 3 = 6 x 1.04^2 + 6 x 1.04 + 6 = 6.4896 + 6.24 + 6 = 18.7296. Sale price at year 3 (2 years remaining, 5.5% yield) = 6 / 1.055 + 106 / 1.055^2 = 100.9232. Total = 18.7296 + 100.9232 = 119.6528. Horizon return = (119.6528 / 104.3295)^(1/3) - 1 = 4.67%, below the original 5% yield because coupons were reinvested at only 4% and yields rose before the sale."},"mistakes":["Thinking that a rise in yields is always bad for a bond investor. For a long horizon, reinvestment gains can outweigh the price loss.","Assuming the YTM equals the realised return for any holding period. It holds only if the bond is held to maturity, there is no default, and coupons are reinvested at the YTM.","Forgetting to price the remaining cash flows at the horizon yield when finding the sale price. The remaining life is the original maturity minus the holding period.","Using Macaulay duration directly as a price sensitivity. Divide by (1 + y / m) to get modified duration first."],"recap":["Bond return = coupons + reinvestment income + price change; YTM assumes a specific reinvestment rate and holding to maturity.","Rising yields: lower price (hurts) but higher reinvestment income (helps); falling yields do the reverse.","Horizon shorter than Macaulay duration: price risk dominates; longer: reinvestment risk dominates; equal: roughly offset.","Modified duration = Macaulay duration / (1 + y/m), and the approximate price change is -ModDur x change in yield.","Interest rate risk is higher for longer maturity, lower coupon, and lower yield level."],"quiz":[{"q":"You buy a 3-year 5% annual-pay bond at par and sell it after exactly one year, immediately after receiving the first coupon. The yield on a 2-year bond at that time is 6%. Your one-year return is closest to:","options":["3.17%","5.00%","1.00%","6.00%"],"answer":0,"why":"Sale price = 5 / 1.06 + 105 / 1.06^2 = 98.17. Return = (5 + 98.17) / 100 - 1 = 3.17%. The rise in yield caused a price loss that outweighed no reinvestment gain."},{"q":"An investor's horizon is longer than a bond's Macaulay duration. A one-time, parallel increase in yields right after purchase will most likely:","options":["Lower the realised return because price risk dominates","Raise the realised return because reinvestment risk dominates","Have no effect on realised return","Raise the price at the horizon"],"answer":1,"why":"When the horizon exceeds Macaulay duration, the gain from reinvesting coupons at higher rates outweighs the lower sale price, so realised return rises."},{"q":"A bond has a Macaulay duration of 4.478 and a yield of 5% (annual compounding). The percentage price change for a 50 bp increase in yield is closest to:","options":["-2.24%","-1.95%","-4.26%","-2.13%"],"answer":3,"why":"Modified duration = 4.478 / 1.05 = 4.265, so the change is about -4.265 x 0.005 = -2.13%. Using Macaulay duration directly gives -2.24%, and -4.26% forgets to multiply by 0.5%."},{"q":"Holding other factors constant, which bond has the greatest interest rate risk?","options":["5-year bond with a 2% coupon","10-year bond with a 2% coupon","10-year bond with an 8% coupon","5-year bond with an 8% coupon"],"answer":1,"why":"Longer maturity and lower coupon both raise duration. The 10-year, 2% bond has the most value concentrated far in the future, so its price is most sensitive."}],"order":10},{"id":"fi-11","topic":"fixed","title":"Yield-Based Bond Duration Measures and Properties","minutes":15,"objective":"Calculate and interpret Macaulay, modified, approximate modified and money duration and PVBP, and explain what drives a bond's duration up or down.","summary":"Duration is the single most used number in fixed income: it tells you roughly how much a bond's price moves when yields move. It is tested with calculations and with 'which bond has the higher duration' questions, and it is how every portfolio manager sizes interest rate risk.","sections":[{"heading":"Duration in plain language","body":["A bond's price falls when its yield rises. Duration measures how sensitive the price is to that move. Two related ideas sit under the one word. **Macaulay duration** is the weighted average time, in years, until you receive the bond's cash flows, where each weight is the present value of that cash flow divided by the bond's price. A bond that pays most of its value late has a long Macaulay duration.","**Modified duration** turns that time measure into a price sensitivity: ModDur = Macaulay duration / (1 + y/m), where y is the annual yield to maturity and m is the number of coupon periods per year. A modified duration of 4.26 means that a 1% (100 bp) rise in yield lowers the price by about 4.26%, and a 1% fall raises it by about 4.26%. The estimate is linear, which is why it is only an approximation for large yield moves (the next lesson fixes this with convexity)."],"bullets":["Macaulay duration is measured in years; modified duration is a percentage price change per 1% yield change.","For a zero-coupon bond there is only one cash flow, so Macaulay duration equals the time to maturity.","For an annual-pay bond, ModDur = Macaulay / (1 + y). For semiannual pay, divide by (1 + y/2) and keep Macaulay in years."]},{"heading":"Approximate modified duration, money duration and PVBP","body":["If you do not want to compute weighted cash flows, reprice the bond twice: once with the yield lowered a little (price PV-) and once with it raised the same amount (price PV+). **Approximate modified duration** is (PV- minus PV+) / (2 x PV0 x change in yield), where PV0 is the starting price. Using a small shift such as 10 bp gives an answer very close to the exact modified duration.","Percentage sensitivity is not the whole story, because a $100 million holding moves more in currency terms than a $1 million one. **Money duration** is modified duration multiplied by the full market value of the position, so it reports the currency loss per 1% (100 bp) yield rise. The **price value of a basis point (PVBP)**, also called BPV or DV01, is the currency change in value for a 1 bp yield change. It can be computed as money duration x 0.0001 or as (PV- minus PV+) / 2 using a 1 bp shift."],"bullets":["Money duration = ModDur x market value of the position (including accrued interest if the full price is used).","PVBP = money duration x 0.0001. It is always quoted as a positive number for a long bond position.","Approximate duration uses the two-sided (central) estimate; this is why it is so accurate."]},{"heading":"What makes duration higher or lower","body":["Duration rises when cash flows are pushed further into the future and falls when they arrive sooner. That gives the standard exam relationships, holding the other variables constant. A longer time to maturity raises duration. A lower coupon rate raises duration, because more of the value sits in the distant principal payment. A lower yield to maturity raises duration, because far-off cash flows are discounted less heavily and so carry more weight.","Two edge cases are worth remembering. A zero-coupon bond has the highest Macaulay duration for its maturity, equal to its maturity. A fixed-rate bond's Macaulay duration is always less than or equal to its maturity (equal only for a zero). Floating-rate notes reset their coupon to market rates, so their price barely reacts to yield changes and their duration is very short, close to the time until the next reset.","Between coupon dates, Macaulay duration falls as time passes, then jumps back up after each coupon is paid, because the next set of cash flows is now a full period further away. Over the whole life of the bond the trend is a gradual decline toward zero as maturity approaches."]},{"heading":"Portfolio duration and its limits","body":["The duration of a bond portfolio can be found two ways. The common approach is the market-value-weighted average of the individual bonds' durations. A 60/40 mix of bonds with durations of 4 and 7 has a portfolio duration of 0.60 x 4 + 0.40 x 7 = 5.2. This is easy and widely used, but it assumes a parallel shift of the yield curve: every bond's yield changes by the same amount.","The second approach computes the portfolio's cash flow yield and the duration of the aggregate cash flows. It is more theoretically sound because it treats the portfolio as one set of cash flows, but it is more work and still relies on a single yield. Weighted-average duration can mislead when yield curve shifts are not parallel; later lessons on curve-based measures address this.","Remember what all yield-based durations assume: the bond's cash flows do not change when yields change. That is fine for option-free bonds. For bonds with embedded options, such as callable bonds, the cash flows themselves depend on the yield, and a different measure (effective duration) is needed."]}],"formulas":["Macaulay duration = sum of [t x PV(cash flow t)] / bond price","Modified duration = Macaulay duration / (1 + y/m)","Approximate modified duration = (PV- - PV+) / (2 x PV0 x change in yield)","Percentage price change = -ModDur x change in yield","Money duration = ModDur x market value of the position","PVBP = (PV- - PV+) / 2 for a 1 bp shift, or money duration x 0.0001","Portfolio duration = sum of (market value weight x bond duration)"],"example":{"title":"Duration measures for a 5-year 6% annual-pay bond","problem":"A 5-year bond pays a 6% annual coupon and yields 5%. Its price is 104.3295 per 100 par. At a 6% yield the price is 100.0000, and a 10 bp shift in yield gives prices of 104.7756 at 4.90% and 103.8858 at 5.10%. Find (a) Macaulay duration, (b) modified duration, (c) approximate modified duration from the 10 bp shift, (d) the PVBP of 10 million par.","solution":"(a) Weighting each cash flow time by its present value share of the price gives a Macaulay duration of 4.4778 years. (b) ModDur = 4.4778 / 1.05 = 4.2645. (c) Approximate ModDur = (104.7756 - 103.8858) / (2 x 104.3295 x 0.001) = 4.2646, essentially the same. (d) Market value of 10 million par = 10,432,948, so money duration = 4.2645 x 10,432,948 = about 44,491,566, and PVBP = 44,491,566 x 0.0001 = about 4,449 per 1 bp. A 100 bp rise in yield would cut the price by roughly 4.26%, and the price change at 6% (4.15%) is a little smaller because of convexity."},"mistakes":["Quoting Macaulay duration when asked for price sensitivity. Divide by (1 + y/m) to get modified duration first.","Forgetting to keep Macaulay duration in years on semiannual bonds. Divide by (1 + y/2), and do not double the number of years.","Reading the duration-yield relationship backwards: a lower yield or lower coupon means higher duration.","Applying weighted-average portfolio duration to non-parallel yield moves, where it can give a wrong answer."],"recap":["Macaulay duration is the weighted average time to cash flows; modified duration converts it to a price sensitivity.","Approximate modified duration reprices the bond up and down by a small yield shift.","Money duration and PVBP give the currency size of the risk, not just a percentage.","Longer maturity, lower coupon and lower yield all raise duration.","Portfolio duration is the market-value-weighted average and assumes parallel yield shifts."],"quiz":[{"q":"A 5-year zero-coupon bond has a yield to maturity of 5% with annual compounding. Its modified duration is closest to:","options":["5.00","4.76","4.55","5.25"],"answer":1,"why":"A zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration equals its maturity, 5.00. ModDur = 5.00 / 1.05 = 4.76. The 5.00 option is the Macaulay duration, which ignores the division by (1 + y)."},{"q":"A bond has a modified duration of 4.26 and trades at 104.33. If its yield rises by 50 bp, the estimated percentage price change is closest to:","options":["-4.26%","+2.13%","-2.13%","-0.43%"],"answer":2,"why":"Change = -4.26 x 0.005 = -0.0213, or -2.13%. The -4.26% answer uses a 100 bp move, and the sign must be negative because yields rose."},{"q":"A portfolio holds 60% of its market value in a bond with modified duration 4 and 40% in a bond with modified duration 7. The weighted-average portfolio duration is:","options":["5.5","5.8","4.9","5.2"],"answer":3,"why":"0.60 x 4 + 0.40 x 7 = 2.4 + 2.8 = 5.2. The 5.5 answer is the simple average, which ignores the market value weights."},{"q":"Holding other factors constant, which bond has the highest duration?","options":["A 20-year bond with a 3% coupon and 5% yield","A 20-year bond with a 7% coupon and 5% yield","A 10-year bond with a 3% coupon and 5% yield","A 10-year bond with a 7% coupon and 5% yield"],"answer":0,"why":"Longer maturity and lower coupon both raise duration. The 20-year 3% bond has the most value concentrated in distant cash flows."}],"order":11},{"id":"fi-12","topic":"fixed","title":"Yield-Based Bond Convexity and Portfolio Properties","minutes":15,"objective":"Calculate approximate convexity, estimate bond price changes using duration plus convexity, and explain how convexity behaves for individual bonds and portfolios.","summary":"Duration alone is a straight-line guess, and bond prices are curved. Convexity corrects that guess, and it is the reason investors prefer positively convex bonds. Expect a calculation using both terms and several conceptual questions on what raises or lowers convexity.","sections":[{"heading":"Why duration is not enough","body":["Plot a bond's price against its yield and you get a curve that bends, not a straight line. Duration is the slope of the line that touches the curve at the current yield. For a small yield change the line and the curve are almost the same. For a large change the line drifts away from the curve, and it always drifts in the same direction for an ordinary bond: the actual price is higher than the line predicts.","That gap is why a duration-only estimate overstates the loss when yields rise and understates the gain when yields fall. **Convexity** measures how much the curve bends, and it is the second-order correction. A bond with positive convexity, which includes every option-free bond, gains more when yields fall than it loses when yields rise by the same amount. This asymmetry is valuable, so investors pay for it in the form of a slightly lower yield."],"bullets":["Duration is the first derivative of price with respect to yield; convexity is the second.","The convexity adjustment is positive for an option-free bond whichever way yields move.","The bigger the yield shift, the more the convexity term matters."]},{"heading":"Approximate convexity and the price change formula","body":["Like duration, convexity can be approximated by repricing the bond with the yield lowered and raised by the same small amount. With PV0 the starting price, PV- the price after a yield decrease and PV+ the price after an increase, **approximate convexity** = (PV- + PV+ - 2 x PV0) / [PV0 x (change in yield)^2]. The numerator is positive when the price curve bends upward.","The full estimate of the percentage price change adds the convexity term to the duration term: **% change in price = -ModDur x change in yield + 0.5 x Convexity x (change in yield)^2**. Because the yield change is squared, the convexity term is the same sign for both rises and falls in yield. Always express the yield change as a decimal (0.01 for 100 bp) and do not forget the one-half."],"bullets":["Convexity units are 'years squared', and typical values are in the tens or even hundreds for long bonds.","Dollar terms: change in value = (-money duration x change in yield) + 0.5 x money convexity x (change in yield)^2, using the position's market value.","Using only the duration term for a 100 bp move on a 10-year bond can miss by several tenths of a percent."]},{"heading":"What drives convexity","body":["Convexity behaves much like duration in the direction of its drivers, but it is more sensitive. A longer maturity, a lower coupon and a lower yield all raise convexity. Convexity also rises when cash flows are more spread out in time, which is called dispersion. That is why a barbell portfolio (holding short and long bonds) has higher convexity than a bullet portfolio (holding bonds clustered around the middle maturity) with the same duration.","Convexity is the reason a callable bond behaves differently. When yields fall, the issuer is likely to call the bond, so the price cannot rise much above the call price. The price curve flattens and then bends the other way, producing **negative convexity** at low yields. A putable bond does the opposite: the put option protects the downside, which adds to its positive convexity. Mortgage-backed securities, covered later, also show negative convexity because borrowers refinance when rates drop."],"bullets":["Higher convexity is desirable, all else equal: it helps in both directions of the yield move.","Callable bonds can have negative convexity; putable bonds have more positive convexity than similar straight bonds.","Zero-coupon bonds have higher convexity than same-maturity coupon bonds."]},{"heading":"Portfolio convexity and effective measures","body":["As with duration, portfolio convexity is the market-value-weighted average of the individual bonds' convexities. If 30% of the value is in a bond with convexity 80 and 70% in a bond with convexity 20, portfolio convexity is 0.30 x 80 + 0.70 x 20 = 38. The same caution applies: the result is only meaningful for roughly parallel yield changes.","The measures in this lesson are built from the bond's yield to maturity, which assumes the cash flows are fixed. They are often called modified duration and modified convexity, or more generally yield-based measures. **Effective duration and effective convexity** use the same repricing formulas but shift the benchmark yield curve rather than the bond's own yield, and they let the cash flows change. This is the right approach for bonds with embedded options, where a yield-based measure can be badly wrong. The next lesson covers effective duration in more detail."]}],"formulas":["Approximate convexity = (PV- + PV+ - 2 x PV0) / [PV0 x (change in yield)^2]","% change in price = -ModDur x change in yield + 0.5 x Convexity x (change in yield)^2","Portfolio convexity = sum of (market value weight x bond convexity)"],"example":{"title":"Duration plus convexity for a 5-year 6% annual-pay bond","problem":"A 5-year 6% annual-pay bond yields 5% and is priced at 104.3295. Its modified duration is 4.2645 and its approximate convexity (from a 10 bp shift) is 23.44. Estimate the percentage price change for a 100 bp rise in yield using (a) duration alone and (b) duration plus convexity. The actual price at a 6% yield is 100.0000.","solution":"(a) Duration only: -4.2645 x 0.01 = -4.26%. (b) Convexity term: 0.5 x 23.44 x 0.01^2 = 0.1172%, so the estimate is -4.2645% + 0.1172% = -4.15%. (c) Actual change: 100.0000 / 104.3295 - 1 = -4.15%. The duration-only estimate overstates the loss; adding convexity brings the estimate to -4.147%, almost exactly the true -4.150%. For a 100 bp fall, the estimate is +4.2645% + 0.1172% = +4.38% against an actual +4.38%."},"mistakes":["Dropping the 0.5 in front of the convexity term, or using 1 instead of 0.01 for a 100 bp move.","Subtracting the convexity term when yields rise. It is always added for a positive-convexity bond.","Assuming callable bonds always have positive convexity. At low yields they can show negative convexity.","Treating higher convexity as a pure cost: all else equal it is an advantage, and the market prices it."],"recap":["Price-yield curves bend, so duration alone is a first-order approximation only.","Approximate convexity = (PV- + PV+ - 2 x PV0) / (PV0 x change in yield squared).","Price change estimate = -ModDur x dy + 0.5 x Convexity x dy^2.","Longer maturity, lower coupon, lower yield and greater cash flow dispersion raise convexity.","Portfolio convexity is the market-value-weighted average; effective measures handle embedded options."],"quiz":[{"q":"A bond has a modified duration of 6 and a convexity of 50. For a 50 bp increase in yield, the estimated percentage price change is closest to:","options":["-3.00%","-3.06%","-2.94%","-2.88%"],"answer":2,"why":"Duration term = -6 x 0.005 = -3.00%. Convexity term = 0.5 x 50 x 0.005^2 = +0.0625%. Total = -2.9375%, about -2.94%. The -3.06% answer subtracts the convexity term, and -2.88% omits the 0.5."},{"q":"Which statement about a bond with positive convexity is correct?","options":["Its price rises more for a yield decrease than it falls for an equal yield increase","Its price rises less for a yield decrease than it falls for an equal yield increase","Its duration estimate understates the price loss when yields rise","Its price changes are the same for equal rises and falls in yield"],"answer":0,"why":"Positive convexity creates the asymmetry. Duration overstates the loss when yields rise, so the duration-only estimate is too pessimistic, not too optimistic."},{"q":"A portfolio has 30% of its market value in a bond with convexity 80 and 70% in a bond with convexity 20. Portfolio convexity is:","options":["50.0","32.0","44.0","38.0"],"answer":3,"why":"0.30 x 80 + 0.70 x 20 = 24 + 14 = 38. The 50 answer is the simple average of 80 and 20."},{"q":"A bond is priced at 100. If yields fall 1% it is worth 104.0, and if yields rise 1% it is worth 96.5. Approximate convexity is closest to:","options":["5.0","50.0","25.0","0.5"],"answer":1,"why":"Convexity = (104.0 + 96.5 - 200) / (100 x 0.01^2) = 0.5 / 0.01 = 50. Forgetting to square the yield change or to include the starting price leads to the other values."}],"order":12},{"id":"fi-13","topic":"fixed","title":"Curve-Based and Empirical Fixed-Income Risk Measures","minutes":15,"objective":"Compute and interpret effective duration, key rate duration and empirical duration, and explain when each is preferred to a yield-based measure.","summary":"Yield-based duration assumes one yield for the bond and fixed cash flows. Real portfolios hold callable bonds and credit bonds, and real yield curves twist. Curve-based and empirical measures handle those problems, and they are tested as short calculations and as 'which measure fits which situation' questions.","sections":[{"heading":"Effective duration: shifting the curve, not the yield","body":["Modified duration starts from the bond's own yield to maturity and assumes the cash flows are fixed. That works for an option-free bond. A callable bond, a putable bond or a mortgage-backed security has cash flows that change when rates change, so a yield-to-maturity measure can be misleading. **Effective duration** fixes this by shifting the benchmark yield curve up and down, repricing the bond with a model that lets the cash flows react, and measuring the price change.","The formula has the same shape as approximate modified duration: EffDur = (PV- - PV+) / (2 x PV0 x change in curve). The difference is what the shift is applied to, and how the prices are produced. The 'change in curve' is a parallel shift in the benchmark curve, and for a bond with an embedded option the two prices come from an option-adjusted pricing model, not from simply discounting fixed cash flows."],"bullets":["Effective duration is the right measure for bonds with embedded options.","For an option-free bond, effective and modified duration are very close.","Callable bonds have effective duration lower than a similar straight bond when yields are low, because the call caps the upside in price.","Effective convexity is computed with the same two repriced values: (PV- + PV+ - 2 x PV0) / [PV0 x (change in curve)^2]."]},{"heading":"Key rate duration: sensitivity along the curve","body":["A single duration number assumes a parallel shift in the yield curve, but curves also steepen, flatten and twist. **Key rate duration** (also called partial duration) measures a bond's sensitivity to a change in one specific maturity point on the benchmark curve, with every other key rate held constant. The 2-year, 5-year, 10-year and 30-year points are common choices, and the rates between them are interpolated.","A bond's key rate durations tell you where its risk sits. A 10-year zero-coupon bond has nearly all its sensitivity at the 10-year key rate. A coupon bond spreads sensitivity across several points, with the biggest share at the maturity where the principal is repaid. The sum of the key rate durations is approximately the effective duration for a parallel shift. For portfolios, key rate durations add up using market-value weights, so a manager can compare a portfolio's exposure with a benchmark at each maturity and see whether it is overweight the long end or underweight the short end."],"bullets":["KRD at one point = (PV- - PV+) / (2 x PV0 x change in that key rate), repricing after moving only that rate.","Total of KRDs is approximately effective duration for a parallel shift.","Use KRDs to measure exposure to curve steepening, flattening or twists, which a single duration cannot do."]},{"heading":"Empirical duration and yield beta","body":["Both effective and yield-based duration are analytical: they come from a pricing model. **Empirical duration** is estimated from observed market data instead, usually by regressing a bond's (or index's) price changes or yield changes on changes in a benchmark yield. It is useful for bonds whose behavior does not match the model, such as high-yield bonds, whose prices respond to the equity market and to credit conditions as well as to Treasury yields.","The key quantity is the **yield beta**, the slope from regressing the bond's yield change on the benchmark's yield change. A yield beta of 0.8 means that a 10 bp move in the Treasury yield has historically been accompanied by an 8 bp move in the bond's yield. A beta below 1 is typical for a credit bond, because credit spreads tend to narrow when Treasury yields rise in a strong economy and widen when they fall. Empirical duration is then analytical duration times yield beta, so a bond with duration 6 and beta 0.8 has an empirical duration of 4.8."],"bullets":["Empirical measures are only as good as the sample period and statistical fit.","They can capture spread-yield interactions that analytical duration ignores.","Think of empirical duration as the 'observed' sensitivity to the benchmark, not the 'theoretical' one."]},{"heading":"Choosing the right measure","body":["A simple decision rule covers most exam questions. For an option-free government or high-grade bond and a small, parallel yield change, modified duration works. For bonds with embedded options or other changing cash flows, use effective duration. If the question mentions a non-parallel shift, a steepening curve or exposure at a particular maturity, use key rate durations. If the question asks about how a bond has actually responded to a benchmark, or about credit bonds whose yields do not move one-for-one with Treasuries, think empirical duration and yield beta.","All of these are still approximations. Each is a first-order estimate, so large moves need convexity too, and the curve-based measures are only as good as the model used to generate the prices."]}],"formulas":["Effective duration = (PV- - PV+) / (2 x PV0 x change in benchmark curve)","Effective convexity = (PV- + PV+ - 2 x PV0) / [PV0 x (change in benchmark curve)^2]","Key rate duration = (PV- - PV+) / (2 x PV0 x change in one key rate)","Sum of key rate durations is approximately effective duration","Empirical duration is approximately analytical duration x yield beta","Yield beta: change in bond yield = alpha + beta x change in benchmark yield"],"example":{"title":"Key rate durations and yield beta","problem":"A bond portfolio has key rate durations of 0.3 at 2 years, 1.1 at 5 years, 2.6 at 10 years and 0.9 at 30 years. (a) What is its effective duration for a parallel shift, and what is the approximate price change for a parallel 25 bp rise? (b) What is the estimated price change if only the 10-year key rate rises by 50 bp? (c) A corporate bond has analytical duration 6.0 and a yield beta of 0.8 to Treasuries. What price change is estimated if Treasury yields rise 25 bp?","solution":"(a) Effective duration is about 0.3 + 1.1 + 2.6 + 0.9 = 4.9. Price change = -4.9 x 0.0025 = -1.225%, about -1.23%. (b) Only the 10-year point moves: -2.6 x 0.005 = -1.30%. (c) Empirical duration = 6.0 x 0.8 = 4.8, so price change = -4.8 x 0.0025 = -1.20%. Ignoring the beta would give -1.50%, overstating the loss."},"mistakes":["Using modified duration for a callable or putable bond. Effective duration is the right measure when the cash flows depend on rates.","Forgetting that key rate durations only describe one point at a time. Moving a single key rate is not a parallel shift.","Dropping the factor of 2 in the denominator of the effective duration formula.","Treating yield beta as a pure number on price. Beta multiplies the yield move, and it is estimated from history, not guaranteed."],"recap":["Effective duration shifts the benchmark curve and lets cash flows change, so it suits embedded-option bonds.","Key rate duration measures exposure to one maturity point, and the KRDs sum to about effective duration.","Use key rate durations when the yield curve shift is non-parallel.","Empirical duration comes from regression; empirical duration is about analytical duration times yield beta.","All of these are first-order estimates; add convexity for large moves."],"quiz":[{"q":"A bond is priced at 100. If the benchmark curve falls 1% the bond is worth 102.5, and if it rises 1% the bond is worth 97.7. Its effective duration is closest to:","options":["4.8","1.2","24.0","2.4"],"answer":3,"why":"EffDur = (102.5 - 97.7) / (2 x 100 x 0.01) = 4.8 / 2 = 2.4. The 4.8 answer omits the 2 in the denominator."},{"q":"Which statement about key rate durations is correct?","options":["The key rate durations of a portfolio sum to approximately its effective duration","A key rate duration measures the effect of a parallel shift of the whole curve","Key rate durations cannot be used for non-parallel curve changes","A zero-coupon bond has key rate durations spread evenly across maturities"],"answer":0,"why":"Moving every key rate by the same amount is a parallel shift, so the sum approximates effective duration. A zero-coupon bond concentrates its sensitivity at its maturity point."},{"q":"A portfolio has a 10-year key rate duration of 2.6 and a total effective duration of 4.9. Only the 10-year benchmark rate rises by 50 bp. The estimated price change is closest to:","options":["-2.45%","-5.20%","-1.30%","-0.26%"],"answer":2,"why":"Only the 10-year key rate moves, so use 2.6: -2.6 x 0.005 = -1.30%. Using total duration 4.9 gives -2.45%, which would apply to a parallel 50 bp shift."},{"q":"A corporate bond has an analytical duration of 6 and a yield beta of 0.8 relative to Treasuries. If Treasury yields rise by 25 bp, the estimated price change is:","options":["-1.50%","-1.20%","-1.88%","-0.30%"],"answer":1,"why":"Empirical duration = 6 x 0.8 = 4.8, so the change is -4.8 x 0.0025 = -1.20%. The -1.50% answer ignores the beta, and -1.88% divides by beta instead of multiplying."}],"order":13},{"id":"fi-14","topic":"fixed","title":"Credit Risk","minutes":15,"objective":"Define credit risk and its components, calculate expected loss, explain how credit spreads, ratings, seniority and recovery relate to each other, and estimate the price effect of a spread change.","summary":"Every bond except a risk-free government bond carries credit risk, and the extra yield investors demand for it is the credit spread. The exam tests the expected loss arithmetic, the meaning of ratings and seniority, and how a spread move changes a price.","sections":[{"heading":"What credit risk is, and its two main parts","body":["**Credit risk** is the risk of loss because a borrower fails to make a promised payment in full and on time. It has two basic building blocks. The first is **default probability** (also called probability of default, POD): the chance, over a stated period, that the issuer fails to pay. The second is **loss severity**, or loss given default (LGD): the share of the exposure that is lost if default happens.","Loss given default is tied to the recovery rate. If bondholders recover 40% of what they are owed, the recovery rate is 40% and LGD is 60% (LGD = 1 - recovery rate). The recovery rate depends on the bond's place in the capital structure, the value of any collateral, and the state of the economy and the issuer's industry at the time of default."],"bullets":["Expected loss = probability of default x loss given default x exposure.","Expected loss is the average cost of credit risk and is built into the bond's price and yield.","Credit risk is not only default: the price can fall merely because credit quality is expected to worsen."]},{"heading":"Credit spread risk and other related risks","body":["The **credit spread** is the extra yield a credit-risky bond offers over a comparable-maturity benchmark, usually a government bond. As a rough guide, the spread compensates for expected loss and for the uncertainty around that loss. A very simple approximation for a one-year horizon is spread ~ POD x LGD. A 2% default probability and 60% LGD imply a spread of about 1.2%. Real spreads are usually larger, because investors also require a risk premium for bearing the uncertainty, and for lower liquidity.","This leads to the main types of credit-related risk. **Credit migration (downgrade) risk** is the risk that the issuer's credit quality falls, which raises the spread investors require and lowers the price even though no default has occurred. **Market liquidity risk** is the risk that a bond can be sold only at a discount to its fair value, and it is usually worse for lower-quality, thinly traded issues, particularly in a crisis. These are often grouped as **spread risk**.","For a small change in spread, the price effect is estimated like any yield change: % change in price ~ -ModDur x change in spread (plus a convexity term for large moves). A bond with modified duration 6 whose spread widens by 50 bp loses about 3.0%."]},{"heading":"Ratings and the investment-grade line","body":["Credit rating agencies assign letter grades that summarise their view of default risk and, in some cases, loss severity. The grades run from AAA (Aaa at Moody's) down through BBB- (Baa3) and then into speculative territory. Bonds rated BBB- / Baa3 or above are **investment grade**; those rated BB+ / Ba1 and below are **high yield** (also called speculative grade or junk). The line matters because many institutions are restricted to investment-grade holdings, so a downgrade across it can force selling.","Agencies rate both the **issuer** and individual **issues**. Because issues in the same company can differ in seniority and security, they can have different ratings. This difference is called notching: a subordinated bond is typically rated one or more notches below the issuer's senior unsecured rating. Ratings are useful and widely followed but they are opinions and can lag the market. They also change slowly, while spreads move continuously, so a bond's spread can signal trouble before an agency acts."],"bullets":["Investment grade: BBB- / Baa3 or higher. High yield: BB+ / Ba1 or lower.","Spreads widen as ratings fall, and the increase is not linear: it jumps sharply for lower grades.","Ratings can also have an outlook (positive, stable, negative) and a watch status."]},{"heading":"Seniority, security and recovery","body":["If an issuer defaults, claims are paid in order of priority. This is the **priority of claims** or seniority ranking. At the top are secured creditors with a claim on specific collateral, followed by unsecured senior creditors, then subordinated creditors, and finally equity holders at the bottom. Claims of the same rank share losses equally, which is called pari passu treatment.","This ordering is the main reason recovery rates differ by bond type. On average, first-lien secured debt recovers the most, senior unsecured less, and subordinated debt the least, although actual outcomes vary by case. Bankruptcy law sometimes departs from strict priority, particularly in negotiated restructurings, so the absolute priority rule is a guide rather than a guarantee. A lower-ranked bond has the same default probability as the issuer but higher loss severity, so it must offer a wider spread."]}],"formulas":["Expected loss = POD x LGD x exposure","LGD = 1 - recovery rate","Approximate credit spread = POD x LGD (before risk premia and liquidity premia)","% change in price ~ -ModDur x change in spread (+ 0.5 x convexity x (change in spread)^2)"],"example":{"title":"Expected loss and spread widening","problem":"An investor holds 10 million of bonds. The one-year probability of default is 2% and the recovery rate in default is 40%. (a) What is the expected loss? (b) What annual credit spread does this imply as a rough guide? (c) If the bond has modified duration 6 and its spread widens by 50 bp, what is the approximate price change?","solution":"(a) LGD = 1 - 0.40 = 60%. Expected loss = 0.02 x 0.60 x 10,000,000 = 120,000. (b) Spread ~ 0.02 x 0.60 = 1.2%, or 120 bp. (c) Price change ~ -6 x 0.005 = -3.0%. Adding a modest convexity correction would reduce the loss slightly, but the duration estimate gives -3.0%, about -300,000 on a 10 million position."},"mistakes":["Using the recovery rate instead of LGD in the expected loss formula. LGD is 1 - recovery rate.","Confusing probability of default with expected loss. Expected loss also depends on severity and exposure.","Placing BB+ / Ba1 inside investment grade. The line is between BBB- / Baa3 and BB+ / Ba1.","Assuming bonds of the same issuer always have the same rating. Seniority and security cause notching."],"recap":["Credit risk has two components: default probability and loss severity.","Expected loss = POD x LGD x exposure, with LGD = 1 - recovery rate.","The credit spread compensates for expected loss and for uncertainty and liquidity.","Investment grade is BBB- / Baa3 and above; downgrades across the line can force selling.","Seniority and security drive recovery: secured > senior unsecured > subordinated > equity."],"quiz":[{"q":"A lender has a 20 million exposure. The probability of default is 3% and the expected recovery rate is 35%. The expected loss is closest to:","options":["210,000","600,000","390,000","13,000,000"],"answer":2,"why":"LGD = 1 - 0.35 = 0.65. Expected loss = 0.03 x 0.65 x 20,000,000 = 390,000. The 210,000 answer uses the recovery rate instead of LGD, and 13,000,000 omits the default probability."},{"q":"A bond with a modified duration of 6 sees its credit spread widen by 50 bp while benchmark yields stay unchanged. The approximate price change is:","options":["-0.30%","-3.00%","-6.00%","+3.00%"],"answer":1,"why":"The spread is part of the bond's yield, so the effect is -6 x 0.005 = -3.00%. A wider spread lowers the price."},{"q":"Which statement about seniority and recovery is correct?","options":["Senior secured debt generally has a higher expected recovery rate than subordinated debt","Subordinated debt generally has a lower default probability than senior debt of the same issuer","Equity holders rank ahead of unsecured creditors in liquidation","Rated issues of the same company must all carry identical ratings"],"answer":0,"why":"Priority of claims puts secured debt first and subordinated debt later. Equity is the residual claim, and notching means different issues can have different ratings."},{"q":"Which statement is incorrect?","options":["Credit migration risk can reduce a bond's price without a default","Market liquidity risk tends to be higher for lower-rated, thinly traded bonds","Loss given default equals one minus the recovery rate","A bond rated BBB- is classified as high yield"],"answer":3,"why":"BBB- (Baa3) is the lowest investment-grade rating. High yield starts at BB+ (Ba1)."}],"order":14},{"id":"fi-15","topic":"fixed","title":"Credit Analysis for Government Issuers","minutes":15,"objective":"Explain the factors used to assess sovereign credit risk, distinguish foreign-currency from local-currency debt, and describe how analysis of sub-sovereign issuers (such as municipalities) differs.","summary":"Governments are the biggest bond issuers, and sovereign debt is not automatically risk-free. The exam asks which factors drive a sovereign's rating and how general obligation and revenue bonds differ, so knowing the framework matters more than memorising any ratios.","sections":[{"heading":"Ability and willingness to pay","body":["A corporation that cannot pay can be taken to court, its assets sold and creditors repaid in order of priority. A sovereign government is different. It has the power to tax, to print its own currency and to change its own laws, and creditors have limited ways to force payment. That is why sovereign credit analysis separates two ideas: **ability to pay** (can the government afford the debt?) and **willingness to pay** (will it choose to honour the debt?).","A government can have the ability but not the willingness, for example after a change of regime or when voters resist austerity. For this reason, political and institutional quality is a central part of the analysis, not an afterthought. Sovereign defaults, when they happen, are usually negotiated restructurings with extended maturities or reduced principal, not liquidation, and creditors often negotiate with bodies such as the IMF in the background."]},{"heading":"The main factors in sovereign credit analysis","body":["Rating agencies and analysts group their work around a handful of areas. The wording varies, but the substance is stable and worth memorising as a framework.","Beyond the factors listed below, analysts look at the structure of government debt. Debt held mostly by domestic investors in local currency is considered more stable than debt held by foreigners in a foreign currency, because domestic holders are less likely to flee and the government can usually service local-currency debt by raising taxes or creating money."],"bullets":["Institutions and governance: rule of law, political stability, quality and predictability of policy, transparency, and track record of honouring debts.","Economic strength: income levels, growth prospects, diversification and the demographic outlook. Larger, richer, faster-growing economies have more capacity to pay.","Fiscal strength: size of debt relative to GDP, the deficit, the interest burden relative to government revenue, and the maturity and currency of debt. A budget deficit that keeps debt growing faster than GDP is a warning sign.","External position: the current account, external debt, and foreign currency reserves. A country that relies on foreign borrowing is vulnerable to sudden stops in funding.","Monetary flexibility: whether the central bank is credible and independent, whether the country has its own currency, and its ability to adjust rates and inflation. Membership in a currency union removes the ability to print money."]},{"heading":"Foreign-currency vs local-currency debt","body":["Rating agencies commonly assign two ratings to a sovereign: one for debt in a foreign currency and one for debt in its own (local) currency. The local-currency rating is often equal to or higher than the foreign-currency rating, because a government can always create its own currency to meet local obligations (at the cost of inflation), while it cannot print dollars or euros. A government that issues mostly in a currency it cannot create loses that safety valve.","There is a related point about debt dynamics. The debt-to-GDP ratio rises when the government runs deficits, and it falls when nominal GDP grows faster than debt. A government with high debt, low growth and an interest rate above nominal growth is on an unstable path unless it runs primary surpluses (a surplus before interest payments)."]},{"heading":"Sub-sovereign (non-sovereign) government issuers","body":["Sub-sovereign issuers are governments below the national level: states, provinces, cities and local authorities, and also agencies. In the United States, bonds issued by these governments are called municipal bonds. They fall into two categories with different analysis.","**General obligation (GO) bonds** are backed by the full faith, credit and taxing power of the issuer, so analysis focuses on the tax base, the economy of the region, the budget, the debt burden and unfunded pension liabilities. **Revenue bonds** are backed only by the cash flows of a specific project or facility, such as a toll road, airport, water utility or hospital, so analysis looks more like corporate credit analysis of that project, including its coverage of debt service by revenues and the strength of the underlying rate or demand.","Sub-sovereigns often depend on the national government for transfers, and the national government may or may not stand behind them. A national support is a credit strength but not a guarantee."],"bullets":["Look for diversified tax base, strong reserves, low debt burden and manageable pension obligations.","Revenue bond analysis asks: Is the project essential? What is its debt service coverage? What are the rate-setting powers?","Legal protections (such as a debt service reserve fund or a rate covenant) can strengthen a revenue bond."]}],"formulas":["Debt-to-GDP = government debt / nominal GDP","Next-year debt-to-GDP ~ (debt + deficit) / (nominal GDP x (1 + nominal growth))"],"example":{"title":"Debt-to-GDP drifting upward","problem":"A government has debt of 600 and nominal GDP of 1,000. It runs a total deficit of 40 for the year (which is added to debt), and nominal GDP grows 4%. What is the debt-to-GDP ratio now and next year, and what does the change suggest?","solution":"Current ratio = 600 / 1,000 = 60.0%. Next year: debt = 600 + 40 = 640 and GDP = 1,000 x 1.04 = 1,040, so the ratio = 640 / 1,040 = 61.5%. The ratio rose by about 1.5 percentage points because the deficit (4% of GDP) was larger than what 4% nominal growth could offset at a 60% starting debt level (growth reduces the ratio by about 2.3 points while the deficit raises it by about 3.8). A weakening fiscal trend like this would be a negative for the rating if it persisted."},"mistakes":["Assuming sovereign debt is risk-free. Governments default, especially on foreign-currency debt.","Ignoring willingness to pay and focusing only on ratios. Political and institutional quality is a core factor.","Treating local-currency and foreign-currency sovereign ratings as identical.","Analysing a revenue bond as if it were backed by the issuer's general taxing power."],"recap":["Sovereign analysis weighs both ability and willingness to pay, because creditors have limited legal recourse.","Key factors: institutions, economy, fiscal strength, external position and monetary flexibility.","Local-currency debt is usually rated at or above foreign-currency debt because the government can create its own currency.","General obligation bonds rest on taxing power; revenue bonds rest on project cash flows.","Debt-to-GDP rises with deficits and falls with nominal growth."],"quiz":[{"q":"A government has debt of 600 and nominal GDP of 1,000. It adds a deficit of 40 to debt, and nominal GDP grows by 4%. Next year's debt-to-GDP ratio is closest to:","options":["57.7%","64.0%","60.0%","61.5%"],"answer":3,"why":"Debt = 640 and GDP = 1,040, so 640 / 1,040 = 61.5%. The 57.7% answer ignores the deficit and 64.0% ignores GDP growth."},{"q":"Why is a sovereign's local-currency debt often rated higher than its foreign-currency debt?","options":["The government can create its own currency to meet local-currency obligations","Local-currency debt always carries a government guarantee","Foreign investors hold most local-currency debt","Local-currency bonds have shorter maturities by law"],"answer":0,"why":"A government can print its own currency (with inflation risk), but not foreign currency, so it has more ability to service debt in its own currency."},{"q":"Which of the following is a revenue bond rather than a general obligation bond?","options":["A bond backed by the full taxing power of a city","A bond backed by a state's general fund","A bond backed by toll collections from a highway","A bond backed by the central government's guarantee"],"answer":2,"why":"Revenue bonds are repaid from the cash flows of a specific project, such as toll revenues. GO bonds rely on taxing power."},{"q":"Which factor in sovereign analysis is mainly about the country's current account, foreign debt and reserves?","options":["Fiscal strength","External position","Institutional effectiveness","Monetary flexibility"],"answer":1,"why":"External position covers the balance of payments, external borrowing and foreign exchange reserves. Fiscal strength concerns the government budget and debt."}],"order":15},{"id":"fi-16","topic":"fixed","title":"Credit Analysis for Corporate Issuers","minutes":15,"objective":"Apply the four Cs, key credit ratios and covenant analysis to a corporate issuer, and explain why bond-level and issuer-level analysis can lead to different conclusions.","summary":"Corporate credit analysis asks one question: will this company generate enough cash to pay its lenders, and what happens to me if it cannot? The exam tests the four Cs framework, the main leverage and coverage ratios, and the impact of seniority and covenants.","sections":[{"heading":"The four Cs","body":["A bondholder's upside is capped at the coupons and principal, but the downside is a loss of capital. So the credit analyst focuses on what could go wrong. A traditional framework organises the work into four Cs.","**Capacity** is the borrower's ability to repay from its operating cash flows. This is the heart of the analysis and covers the industry (growth, cyclicality, competition), the company's competitive position, its management and strategy, and its financial ratios. **Collateral** looks at the quality and value of the assets, and whether any assets specifically back the debt. **Covenants** are the contractual terms that protect lenders. **Character** is the quality of management and its track record of treating creditors well, including the quality of its financial reporting and governance."],"bullets":["Capacity: cash flow generation, margins, industry position, leverage.","Collateral: asset quality, value in a liquidation, and which assets are pledged.","Covenants: legal limits and obligations, discussed below.","Character: management's integrity, its strategy, and its record in prior downturns."]},{"heading":"Key credit ratios","body":["Analysts use two families of ratios. **Leverage ratios** show how much debt the company carries relative to what it earns. Common choices are debt / EBITDA, net debt / EBITDA (net debt = debt minus cash), debt / capital, and funds from operations (FFO) / debt. **Coverage ratios** show how comfortably earnings cover the interest bill: EBITDA / interest expense and EBIT / interest expense. Higher coverage and lower leverage signal stronger credit.","Cash-flow ratios are also used because earnings are not cash. Free operating cash flow / debt (cash from operations less capital spending, relative to debt) shows how quickly debt could be repaid from internally generated funds. No single ratio is enough, and the ratios have to be compared with peers and against the issuer's own history. Ratios that look fine at the top of the cycle can deteriorate sharply in a downturn, so analysts look at cyclical industries on a through-the-cycle basis and test how ratios behave under stress."],"bullets":["EBITDA ignores depreciation, so EBIT coverage is the stricter test for capital-intensive firms.","Higher-rated issuers typically show lower debt / EBITDA and higher interest coverage; the exact thresholds differ by industry.","Off-balance-sheet obligations such as leases and pensions may need to be added back to debt when comparing issuers."]},{"heading":"Covenants and bond-level analysis","body":["**Covenants** are the promises written into the bond indenture. **Affirmative covenants** say what the issuer must do: make payments on time, maintain insurance, file financial statements, keep assets in good condition. **Negative covenants** say what it must not do, such as taking on additional debt above a limit, selling key assets, paying excessive dividends, or pledging assets to other lenders ahead of the bondholders. Breaching a covenant normally lets bondholders demand remedies, and that gives them a seat at the table early, before the company is in real trouble.","Covenants matter more for lower-rated issuers. Stronger covenants lower a bond's risk and so lower the yield the issuer must pay; weaker covenants do the opposite. This is why covenant quality is a distinct part of analysis and not simply a legal detail."]},{"heading":"Issuer-level vs bond-level: seniority and structure","body":["Credit analysis is done at two levels. The **issuer-level** view (the issuer credit rating) assesses the company's overall ability to pay. The **bond-level** view then adjusts for the specific bond's position in the claims waterfall. A senior secured bond is better protected than the same company's subordinated bond, so the two have different expected recoveries and are rated differently, with senior secured above senior unsecured and subordinated below.","Structure can also hurt unsecured holders of a parent company. A holding company's creditors are repaid only from what is left after the operating subsidiaries pay their own creditors, because the subsidiary's lenders have the first claim on its assets. This is **structural subordination**. A parent-level bond can therefore carry a lower rating and a wider spread than a bond issued by its operating subsidiary, even though both belong to the same group. Analysts should always check which entity issued the bond and where the assets and cash flows sit."]}],"formulas":["Debt / EBITDA; net debt / EBITDA, where net debt = debt - cash","Interest coverage = EBITDA / interest expense, or EBIT / interest expense","FFO / debt; free operating cash flow / debt"],"example":{"title":"Leverage and coverage for a corporate issuer","problem":"A company has debt of 600, cash of 60, EBITDA of 150, EBIT of 110 and interest expense of 30. Calculate debt / EBITDA, net debt / EBITDA, EBITDA coverage and EBIT coverage.","solution":"Debt / EBITDA = 600 / 150 = 4.0x. Net debt = 600 - 60 = 540, so net debt / EBITDA = 540 / 150 = 3.6x. EBITDA coverage = 150 / 30 = 5.0x. EBIT coverage = 110 / 30 = 3.67x. The EBIT figure is lower because depreciation is a real cost of keeping the business running, and a capital-intensive company should be judged on the stricter ratio."},"mistakes":["Using EBITDA coverage alone for a capital-intensive firm. EBIT coverage is stricter.","Adding cash to debt when computing net debt. Net debt is debt minus cash.","Assuming all of an issuer's bonds have the same credit risk. Seniority and security change recovery.","Treating a parent holding company's bond as equal in quality to a subsidiary's bond."],"recap":["Four Cs: capacity, collateral, covenants and character.","Leverage ratios (debt / EBITDA) and coverage ratios (EBITDA / interest) are the core metrics.","Affirmative covenants require actions; negative covenants restrict actions.","Issuer-level and bond-level analysis differ because of seniority, security and structure.","Structural subordination weakens parent-level creditors relative to subsidiary creditors."],"quiz":[{"q":"A company has debt of 600, cash of 60 and EBITDA of 150. Net debt / EBITDA is closest to:","options":["4.0x","3.6x","4.4x","0.25x"],"answer":1,"why":"Net debt = 600 - 60 = 540 and 540 / 150 = 3.6x. The 4.0x answer is gross debt / EBITDA, and 4.4x wrongly adds the cash."},{"q":"A company has EBIT of 110, EBITDA of 150 and interest expense of 30. Its EBIT interest coverage is closest to:","options":["5.00x","4.00x","0.27x","3.67x"],"answer":3,"why":"110 / 30 = 3.67x. The 5.00x answer uses EBITDA, and 0.27x inverts the ratio."},{"q":"A covenant that limits the issuer's ability to take on additional debt is best described as:","options":["A negative covenant","An affirmative covenant","A collateral provision","A character assessment"],"answer":0,"why":"Negative covenants restrict actions such as incurring more debt. Affirmative covenants require actions such as paying on time."},{"q":"Why might a bond issued by a holding company have a lower credit rating than a bond issued by its operating subsidiary?","options":["Holding companies are always smaller","Holding company bonds are secured","Structural subordination: subsidiary creditors have the first claim on subsidiary assets","Subsidiary bonds have no covenants"],"answer":2,"why":"The holding company receives value only after the subsidiary's own creditors are paid, so its creditors are effectively subordinated."}],"order":16},{"id":"fi-17","topic":"fixed","title":"Fixed-Income Securitization","minutes":15,"objective":"Describe the securitization process, the roles of the parties involved, how tranching and credit enhancement redistribute risk, and the benefits and risks of securitization.","summary":"Securitization turns pools of loans into tradable bonds, and it is behind most of the mortgage, auto loan and credit card funding in the world. The exam tests the parties, the special purpose vehicle, the waterfall and credit enhancement, and it often includes a simple loss-allocation calculation.","sections":[{"heading":"What securitization is and who is involved","body":["**Securitization** is the process by which a company that owns a group of loans or receivables sells them to a separate legal entity, which pays for them by issuing bonds backed by the cash flows from that pool. The loans can be mortgages, car loans, credit card balances or student loans. Investors in the bonds are paid from the interest and principal that the borrowers pay on the underlying loans, not from the credit of the company that originally made them.","The main parties are straightforward. The **originator** (or seller) created the loans, for example a bank, and sells them to the issuer. The **issuer** is a **special purpose vehicle (SPV)**, also called a special purpose entity (SPE) or special purpose company, set up solely to buy the loans and issue the securities. The **servicer** collects payments from borrowers, chases late payers and passes the cash on. The **trustee** holds the legal interest on behalf of the investors. Other participants include rating agencies, underwriters and sometimes a credit enhancement provider."],"bullets":["Originator: creates the loans and sells them to the SPV.","SPV / SPE: legally separate issuer of the asset-backed securities.","Servicer: collects and distributes payments, and manages delinquencies.","Trustee: oversees the transaction and protects investors' rights."]},{"heading":"Why the SPV matters: true sale and bankruptcy remoteness","body":["The loans must be transferred to the SPV in a **true sale**, meaning the originator no longer owns them. This creates **bankruptcy remoteness**: if the originator later goes bankrupt, its creditors cannot reach the loans, because they now belong to the SPV. Investors can therefore judge the bonds on the quality of the loan pool, rather than on the originator's financial health. That is what allows the bonds to receive a credit rating higher than the originator's own, and it is the central legal idea in securitization.","The SPV is usually independent, with its own directors, and it is restricted to the securitization activity. The originator receives cash up front for the loans, which it can use to make new loans."]},{"heading":"Tranching and the waterfall","body":["A pool of loans produces one stream of cash flows, but investors have different risk appetites. **Tranching** divides the securities into slices with different priority. Senior tranches are paid first and absorb losses last. Mezzanine (middle) tranches come next. The most junior tranche, often called the equity or residual tranche, is paid last and absorbs losses first, so it offers the highest yield and the highest risk. The rule that determines the order of payment and loss is called the **waterfall**.","Consider a 100 pool split into a 70 senior tranche, a 20 mezzanine tranche and a 10 equity tranche. If the pool loses 12 in total, the equity tranche absorbs the first 10 and is wiped out, the mezzanine absorbs the next 2 (10% of its balance) and the senior tranche is untouched. If losses reach 25, the equity is gone, the mezzanine loses 15 of its 20, and the senior tranche is still intact. The senior tranche starts to lose money only when pool losses exceed 30."]},{"heading":"Credit enhancement and benefits","body":["**Credit enhancement** reduces the credit risk of the securities. **Internal** credit enhancement comes from the structure of the deal itself. Subordination is the tranche structure described above: the senior tranche in the example has 30 of subordination beneath it. **Overcollateralization** means the pool is bigger than the securities issued (for example, a 105 pool backing 100 of bonds), so the extra 5 absorbs losses first. **Excess spread** is the extra interest the pool earns over what the bonds and fees cost, which can be used to cover losses or build a **reserve account**, a cash buffer.","**External** credit enhancement comes from a third party: a surety bond, a financial guarantee, or a letter of credit. It carries the risk that the provider itself could be downgraded or fail.","Securitization benefits many parties. Originators get funding, often at a lower cost, and they free capital to make more loans. Investors gain access to assets and risk profiles that were not otherwise available, in tailored tranches. The wider market gets more liquidity and a more efficient allocation of credit. The risks include model and rating risk, the weak incentives of originators who no longer hold the loan risk, and complexity."],"bullets":["Internal: subordination, overcollateralization, excess spread, reserve accounts.","External: surety bonds, guarantees, letters of credit.","Credit enhancement raises the rating of senior tranches above the quality of the average loan."]}],"formulas":[],"example":{"title":"Loss allocation in a three-tranche structure","problem":"A securitization pool of 100 is split into a senior tranche of 70, a mezzanine tranche of 20 and an equity tranche of 10. How is a total pool loss of 25 allocated, and what is the subordination protecting the senior tranche?","solution":"Losses are absorbed from the bottom. The equity tranche takes the first 10 (all of it). The remaining 15 falls on the mezzanine tranche, which loses 15 of its 20 (75%) and has 5 left. The senior tranche loses nothing. The subordination beneath the senior tranche is 20 + 10 = 30, or 30% of the pool, so the senior tranche is only hit when pool losses exceed 30."},"mistakes":["Thinking the originator's credit quality determines the rating of the securities. With a true sale, the loan pool does.","Reversing the order of losses: junior tranches absorb losses first and senior tranches last.","Counting external guarantees as internal enhancement. External support comes from a third party.","Assuming that securitization removes credit risk. It redistributes it among investors."],"recap":["Securitization moves loans to a bankruptcy-remote SPV that issues securities backed by the loan cash flows.","Key parties: originator, SPV, servicer and trustee.","Tranching sets the order of payment and loss; junior tranches bear losses first.","Internal enhancement: subordination, overcollateralization, excess spread, reserve accounts. External: guarantees, surety bonds, letters of credit.","Benefits include lower funding cost, liquidity and tailored risk; risks include complexity and weak originator incentives."],"quiz":[{"q":"A pool of 100 is split into a 70 senior, 20 mezzanine and 10 equity tranche. If the pool suffers losses of 25, the loss borne by the mezzanine tranche is:","options":["10","15","20","25"],"answer":1,"why":"The equity tranche absorbs the first 10, leaving 15 for the mezzanine tranche. The mezzanine tranche keeps 5 of its 20, and the senior tranche is unaffected."},{"q":"In the same structure (70 senior, 20 mezzanine, 10 equity), the subordination protecting the senior tranche is:","options":["10%","20%","30%","70%"],"answer":2,"why":"The mezzanine and equity tranches total 30, which is 30% of the 100 pool. Losses must exceed 30 before the senior tranche is hit."},{"q":"Which of the following is an external form of credit enhancement?","options":["Subordination","Overcollateralization","Excess spread","A letter of credit from a bank"],"answer":3,"why":"A letter of credit comes from a third party. The other three come from the structure of the deal itself."},{"q":"The main reason the loans are sold to a special purpose vehicle in a true sale is to:","options":["Make the securities independent of the originator's bankruptcy risk","Increase the interest paid by the borrowers","Transfer the servicing duties to the investors","Remove the need for credit enhancement"],"answer":0,"why":"A true sale makes the pool bankruptcy remote, so creditors of the originator cannot reach it and investors can focus on the loans themselves."}],"order":17},{"id":"fi-18","topic":"fixed","title":"Asset-Backed Security (ABS) Instrument and Market Features","minutes":15,"objective":"Describe the main types of ABS collateral, distinguish amortizing from non-amortizing structures, and explain excess spread, early amortization and the prepayment and credit risks of ABS.","summary":"Auto loans, credit card receivables and student loans are all securitized into ABS, and each behaves differently. The exam focuses on the contrast between amortizing and revolving pools, on excess spread, and on what risks matter for each collateral type.","sections":[{"heading":"What ABS are and what backs them","body":["An **asset-backed security (ABS)** is a bond backed by a pool of loans or receivables other than residential mortgages (which are covered in the next lesson). The securitization mechanics are the ones from the previous lesson: an originator sells the pool to a special purpose vehicle, which issues tranched securities. What changes from one ABS to another is the collateral and the way its cash flows behave.","Common collateral includes auto loans and leases, credit card receivables, student loans, equipment loans and leases, and small business loans. Another important group is the collateralized debt obligation (CDO) family, such as collateralized loan obligations (CLOs), where the pool is made up of corporate loans or bonds and is actively managed. In a CLO the manager can buy and sell loans within limits, so the quality of the manager matters as well as the collateral."]},{"heading":"Amortizing vs non-amortizing ABS","body":["The first big distinction is whether the underlying loans repay principal over time. Auto loan pools and many student loan pools are **amortizing**: every monthly payment includes interest and some principal, so the pool balance shrinks steadily. The principal paid by the borrowers (scheduled payments plus any prepayments) flows through to the ABS investors, and the securities amortize too.","Credit card receivables are different. Card balances are **non-amortizing**: they have no fixed schedule, borrowers can revolve their balance, and new charges are added. These ABS typically have a **revolving period** (also called a lockout period) during which principal collections are reinvested in new receivables rather than paid to investors, so investors receive only interest and the balance of the security stays unchanged. After that comes an **amortization period** in which principal is passed on to investors, either gradually (controlled amortization) or in a lump sum (bullet repayment). This is why credit card ABS often have a defined expected maturity even though the underlying cards have no maturity."],"bullets":["Amortizing: auto loans, student loans, equipment loans. Principal repaid steadily.","Non-amortizing: credit card receivables. Revolving period, then amortization.","Early amortization (a rapid payout) is triggered if the pool deteriorates, for example if excess spread turns negative, delinquencies rise, or the credit enhancement falls too low."]},{"heading":"Excess spread","body":["**Excess spread** is the first line of defence in most ABS. It is the interest the pool generates minus everything that has to be paid out of it: the coupons on the securities, the servicing and trustee fees, and the loans written off as losses during the period. It is usually expressed as a percentage of the pool. A positive excess spread can absorb credit losses, be trapped in a reserve account, or be released to the residual holder.","Take a pool of 200 million yielding 9% (18 million in interest). Securities of 200 million with a 5% weighted coupon cost 10 million, servicing at 1% costs 2 million and charge-offs of 2% cost 4 million. Excess spread is 18 - 10 - 2 - 4 = 2 million, or 1% of the pool. If charge-offs rose to 4% (8 million), excess spread would be -2 million: the pool no longer covers its costs, credit enhancement must fill the gap, and an early amortization trigger might be hit."]},{"heading":"Prepayment and other risks","body":["Prepayment risk matters for amortizing pools, because investors do not know how quickly principal will come back. For auto loans the effect is usually modest. Borrowers have little incentive to refinance for a small rate saving, and early payoffs come mainly from sales, trade-ins and defaults, not interest rate moves. Prepayments are therefore much less sensitive to interest rates than for residential mortgages. Credit card ABS have no prepayment risk in the same sense, because principal is reinvested during the revolving period, but early amortization shortens their life in a downturn. Some student and home equity pools do carry prepayment features.","Credit risk is the larger concern in most ABS and depends on the collateral: loan-to-value ratios and borrower credit scores for auto loans, charge-off and payment rates for credit cards, and, for CLOs, the diversification and the manager's skill. Other risks include servicer risk (the servicer failing or performing poorly), counterparty risk from external enhancement providers, and legal risk concerning the true sale. The senior tranches are protected by the subordination and enhancement described in the securitization lesson, so most ABS sold to investors in the senior tranches are rated investment grade."],"bullets":["Auto loan ABS: weighted average life shortened by prepayments and defaults; credit risk tied to borrower quality and used-car values.","Credit card ABS: key metrics are charge-offs, payment rate and yield on the receivables.","CLOs: actively managed corporate loan pools with tranched notes and manager risk."]}],"formulas":["Excess spread = interest collected - securities' coupons - servicing and other fees - charge-offs"],"example":{"title":"Excess spread in a loan pool","problem":"A pool of 200 million earns a 9% yield. The securities have a total balance of 200 million and a weighted coupon of 5%. Servicing and trustee fees are 1% of the pool, and charge-offs are 2% of the pool. Calculate the annual excess spread, and recalculate if charge-offs rise to 4%.","solution":"Interest collected = 0.09 x 200 = 18 million. Securities' coupons = 0.05 x 200 = 10 million. Fees = 0.01 x 200 = 2 million. Charge-offs = 0.02 x 200 = 4 million. Excess spread = 18 - 10 - 2 - 4 = 2 million, or 1.0% of the pool. With 4% charge-offs (8 million), excess spread = 18 - 10 - 2 - 8 = -2 million, or -1.0%, so credit enhancement would be drawn on and an early amortization trigger might be tripped."},"mistakes":["Treating all ABS as amortizing. Credit card ABS are non-amortizing and have a revolving period.","Assuming auto loan prepayments respond to interest rates as strongly as mortgage prepayments do.","Forgetting to deduct charge-offs when calculating excess spread.","Thinking early amortization is a prepayment choice by borrowers. It is triggered by the deal's terms when performance deteriorates."],"recap":["ABS are backed by non-mortgage receivables such as auto loans, credit cards and student loans; CLOs are managed loan pools.","Auto loan ABS amortize; credit card ABS are non-amortizing with a revolving period followed by amortization.","Excess spread = pool interest minus coupons, fees and charge-offs, and it is the first buffer for losses.","Early amortization events protect investors by ending the revolving period when performance weakens.","Prepayment risk is mild in auto ABS; credit risk and servicer risk are more important."],"quiz":[{"q":"A pool of 100 million yields 8%. The securities cost 4.5 million in coupons, fees are 0.5 million and charge-offs are 1.5 million. Annual excess spread is:","options":["3.5 million","3.0 million","1.5 million","2.0 million"],"answer":2,"why":"Interest = 8 million. Excess spread = 8 - 4.5 - 0.5 - 1.5 = 1.5 million. The 3.5 million answer ignores fees and charge-offs, and 3.0 million forgets to deduct the charge-offs."},{"q":"Which collateral type is typically non-amortizing and has a revolving period?","options":["Auto loans","Student loans","Equipment loans","Credit card receivables"],"answer":3,"why":"Card balances revolve with no fixed schedule, so principal is reinvested during the revolving period, followed by amortization."},{"q":"Which event would most likely cause a credit card ABS to enter early amortization?","options":["Interest rates on Treasuries falling slightly","Excess spread turning negative as charge-offs rise","The servicer reporting on time","Cardholders paying balances in full"],"answer":1,"why":"Early amortization triggers are performance-based, such as negative excess spread, rising delinquencies or insufficient credit enhancement."},{"q":"Compared with residential mortgage pools, prepayments in auto loan pools are generally:","options":["Less sensitive to interest rates, driven more by sales, trade-ins and defaults","More sensitive to interest rates because of refinancing","Irrelevant, since auto loans cannot be repaid early","Always faster, since car loans are short"],"answer":0,"why":"The savings from refinancing a small auto loan are modest, so early payoffs come mainly from vehicle sales, trade-ins and defaults."}],"order":18},{"id":"fi-19","topic":"fixed","title":"Mortgage-Backed Security (MBS) Instrument and Market Features","minutes":15,"objective":"Describe mortgage loans and mortgage pass-through securities, explain prepayment measures and prepayment risk, and distinguish contraction and extension risk, CMO structures and agency from non-agency MBS.","summary":"Mortgage-backed securities are among the largest bond markets in the world, and their defining feature is that borrowers can repay early. That creates risks and structures you will not see in ordinary bonds, and they are a common source of Level I questions.","sections":[{"heading":"Mortgage loans and pass-through securities","body":["A **mortgage** is a loan secured by real estate. The standard residential loan in the United States is a fixed-rate, level-payment, fully amortizing loan: each monthly payment is the same, early payments are mostly interest, and later payments are mostly principal. Other loans have rates that adjust with a reference rate (adjustable-rate mortgages) or have a balloon payment due at maturity. The **loan-to-value (LTV) ratio**, the loan amount divided by the property value, is a key measure of credit risk: the lower the LTV, the bigger the borrower's equity cushion. Many residential mortgages are non-recourse, meaning the lender can take only the property and not the borrower's other assets.","The borrower has an option that ordinary bonds do not: the right to repay all or part of the loan early, with no penalty in most markets. In a **mortgage pass-through security**, many loans are pooled and investors receive a pro rata share of all the pool's payments: scheduled interest, scheduled principal and prepayments, minus servicing and guarantee fees. As a result, the pass-through rate paid to investors is lower than the weighted average coupon (WAC) of the underlying loans. The pool's weighted average maturity (WAM) and WAC describe it, and the **weighted average life (WAL)** is the average time until principal is received, which depends on prepayments."]},{"heading":"Prepayment measures: SMM, CPR and PSA","body":["The **single monthly mortality rate (SMM)** is the fraction of a pool's remaining balance, after scheduled principal, that is prepaid in a given month. The **conditional prepayment rate (CPR)** is the same thing annualised. They are linked by SMM = 1 - (1 - CPR)^(1/12). A CPR of 6% gives an SMM of about 0.514% per month, not 0.5%, because of compounding. For a pool with 100 million balance and 0.1 million of scheduled principal, the month's prepayment is 0.514% x 99.9 million, about 0.514 million.","A single CPR does not describe real pools, because new loans prepay slowly at first and speed up as they age (seasoning). The **PSA benchmark** captures this: at 100 PSA, CPR starts at 0.2% in month 1 and rises by 0.2 percentage points each month until it reaches 6% in month 30, and then stays at 6%. A pool at 150 PSA prepays at 1.5 times those rates (so 9% after month 30), and 50 PSA at half. A faster PSA means faster prepayments."],"bullets":["Rates fall: refinancing incentive rises, prepayments rise.","Other drivers: housing turnover, seasoning, burnout (the pool's remaining borrowers are the least responsive) and the economy.","SMM is monthly; CPR is annual; PSA is a speed relative to a standard curve."]},{"heading":"Prepayment risk: contraction and extension","body":["Prepayment creates two linked risks. **Contraction risk** arises when interest rates fall: borrowers refinance, principal is returned early, and investors must reinvest at lower rates. The security's life shortens exactly when investors would have liked to keep their higher coupon. **Extension risk** arises when rates rise: prepayments slow, the security's life extends, and investors are stuck with a below-market coupon for longer. Both hurt investors, and the result is **negative convexity**: the price rises less than a straight bond when rates fall (as the price is capped by prepayments) and falls as much or more when rates rise.","Notice that MBS investors are effectively short a call option on the mortgages, which is given to the borrowers. The compensation is a higher yield than a Treasury of similar maturity."]},{"heading":"CMOs, agency and non-agency MBS","body":["A **collateralized mortgage obligation (CMO)** redistributes the pool's cash flows and prepayment risk among tranches. In a **sequential-pay** CMO, all principal goes to the first tranche until it is retired, then to the next. Early tranches have short, more predictable lives (more contraction risk), and later tranches have longer lives (more extension risk). A **planned amortization class (PAC)** tranche is paid on a schedule that holds as long as prepayments stay within a band. The **support tranches** absorb the excess or shortfall, so they have the most prepayment risk. Other classes include floating-rate tranches and stripped securities, where interest-only (IO) and principal-only (PO) strips divide the interest and principal.","In the **agency** market, securities are issued or guaranteed by Ginnie Mae (explicit backing of the US government), or by Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac (government-sponsored enterprises). They carry little credit risk, so the key risk is prepayment. **Non-agency** MBS have no such guarantee, so they depend on credit enhancement (subordination, overcollateralization and so on) and credit risk matters. **Commercial MBS (CMBS)** are backed by loans on commercial property such as offices and malls. These loans are usually non-recourse, often balloon loans, and typically have call protection through prepayment penalties or lockouts, so prepayment risk is much lower, but analysts focus on the debt service coverage ratio and LTV of the property."]}],"formulas":["SMM = 1 - (1 - CPR)^(1/12)","Prepayment in a month = SMM x (beginning balance - scheduled principal)","100 PSA: CPR = 0.2% x month number up to month 30, then 6%; X PSA = X/100 x the 100 PSA CPR"],"example":{"title":"From CPR to prepayment dollars, and PSA speeds","problem":"A mortgage pool has a balance of 100 million at the start of the month, with scheduled principal of 0.1 million. The pool prepays at a CPR of 6%. (a) What is the SMM and the month's prepayment? (b) At 150 PSA, what is the CPR in month 20 and in month 40?","solution":"(a) SMM = 1 - (1 - 0.06)^(1/12) = 0.5143%. Prepayment = 0.005143 x (100 - 0.1) million = about 0.5138 million. (b) At 100 PSA the month-20 CPR is 0.2% x 20 = 4.0%, so at 150 PSA it is 4.0% x 1.5 = 6.0%. In month 40 the 100 PSA CPR has levelled off at 6%, so 150 PSA gives 6% x 1.5 = 9.0%."},"mistakes":["Dividing CPR by 12 to get SMM. Use SMM = 1 - (1 - CPR)^(1/12).","Forgetting that PSA rises for 30 months before levelling off at 6% CPR at 100 PSA.","Mixing up the risks: falling rates cause contraction risk, rising rates cause extension risk.","Assuming agency MBS are free of risk. They have little credit risk but still have prepayment risk."],"recap":["Pass-throughs pass on interest, scheduled principal and prepayments, minus servicing and guarantee fees.","SMM = 1 - (1 - CPR)^(1/12); PSA is a speed measured relative to a standard seasoning curve.","Falling rates bring contraction risk; rising rates bring extension risk; MBS show negative convexity.","CMOs (sequential, PAC and support tranches) redistribute prepayment risk.","Agency MBS have little credit risk, non-agency MBS rely on credit enhancement, and CMBS have call protection."],"quiz":[{"q":"A pool prepays at 150 PSA. The CPR in month 20 is closest to:","options":["4.0%","6.0%","9.0%","3.0%"],"answer":1,"why":"At 100 PSA the CPR in month 20 is 0.2% x 20 = 4.0%. At 150 PSA it is 1.5 x 4.0% = 6.0%. The 9.0% answer applies 150 PSA to the 6% plateau, which only applies after month 30."},{"q":"Interest rates fall sharply. Which risk is most relevant for investors in a mortgage pass-through security?","options":["Contraction risk, as prepayments speed up","Extension risk, as prepayments slow down","No prepayment risk, since the coupon is fixed","Default risk of the agency guarantor"],"answer":0,"why":"Falling rates lead to refinancing, faster prepayments and a shorter life, and investors must reinvest at lower rates. That is contraction risk."},{"q":"In a CMO structure with PAC and support tranches, the tranche that bears the most prepayment risk is generally the:","options":["PAC tranche","Agency guarantor","Support tranche","First tranche in a sequential-pay structure only"],"answer":2,"why":"Support tranches absorb the variability in prepayments so that the PAC tranche's schedule can be maintained, which makes them the riskiest."},{"q":"A pool prepays at a CPR of 12%. The SMM is closest to:","options":["1.00%","0.50%","12.0%","1.06%"],"answer":3,"why":"SMM = 1 - (1 - 0.12)^(1/12) = 1.06%. Dividing CPR by 12 gives 1.00%, which ignores compounding."}],"order":19}];
